    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Senior-Academic-Advisor_R-096834-2": {
      "title": "Senior Academic Advisor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Title Senior Academic Advisor Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department Academic Advising Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Staff Job Description TAMU-CC is a dynamic university designated as both a Hispanic-Serving Institution (HSI) and Minority-Serving Institution (MSI) with approximately 11,000 students from 47 states and 54 foreign nations. We employ over 1,400 full-time and 2,000 part-time Islanders (including students/GAs). The University attracts highly talented faculty and staff and offers an array of undergraduate and graduate degrees, including doctoral programs.  As a member of the Texas A&M University System, TAMU-CC benefits from a range of resources, increased visibility and influence, and opportunities to collaborate in mutually beneficial ways with peers across member institutions and associated agencies. TAMU-CC’s beautiful campus is located on a 240-acre island on Corpus Christi Bay and was ranked #1 College by the Sea by Best College Reviews. Our natural setting is enhanced by its modern, attractive, and state-of-the-art classroom buildings and support facilities. PURPOSE The Senior Academic Advisor reports to the Assistant Director of Academic Advising and provides expert guidance to students, helping them navigate academic requirements, select courses, and achieve their educational goals. This role involves strategic planning, mentorship, and personalized advising to support student success. The Senior Academic Advisor also leads and mentors’ junior advisors, contributes to advising strategies, and uses data to improve student retention. By fostering an environment of academic excellence, this position supports the institution’s mission to enhance student achievement. The Senior Academic Advisor complies with all state, system, and university policies, regulations, rules, procedures, guidelines, and directives in a timely manner and adhere to confidentiality policies (FERPA). RESPONSIBILITIES Advising : 50% Provides academic advising for students grounded in an advanced understanding of the tenets of practice outlined by the National Association of Academic Advising (NACADA) and appropriate academic advising and student development theories. Advises for course selection, registration, academic planning, career and educational goal setting, appropriate resources, transitioning to the university, and retention issues. Performs administrative tasks independently that are associated with assigned advising function. Advises in both group and individual settings with current and prospective students. Interprets State, University, and college policies and procedures. Maintains accurate records of student contacts; may assist in the development of systems, processes, or policies for maintaining records of student contacts. Monitors academic progress and implements intervention strategies for students who receive academic alerts or who need assistance in maintaining or improving their academic standing. Promotes student learning, development, and personal growth to empower self-sufficiency. Provides students with proactive referrals to appropriate resources and timely reminders, and other targeted individual and group communications using email, texting, and social media. Support : 25% Manages/coordinates advising projects, events, outreach, and interventions for all students. Actively seeks to identify individual, organizational, and environmental conditions that foster or inhibit student success. Prepares various academic and advising reports, data analysis of advising initiatives, serves as an ombudsperson to facilitate the application, admission, and retention of students. Support retention efforts include using Navigate and requiring probationary and returning suspension/enforce withdrawal students to schedule an appointment and discuss university guidelines and expectations Leads workshops, seminars and other student engagement activities across campus. May lead and/or oversee special projects specific to the academic college or advising center. Leadership : 25% Collaborates with members of the campus community in student retention initiatives. Leads campus partners in designing and implementing strategic, campus-wide programs and initiatives to support student retention and degree completion goals. Serves on committees to advocate for and contribute ideas to promote a positive student experience across campus initiatives. Conducts research and publishes or presents on best practices and theory within the field of academic advising, students in transition, and related topics to foster professional growth and improve the student advising experience. Trains professional standards and competencies of academic advising to the advising community. Demonstrates mastery of advising tools and strategies, campus resources, and relevant updates May supervise staff and/or student employees to assist with administrative advising duties and programming. QUALIFICATIONS Master’s Degree Five (5) years of related experience. Additional experience/education may be used as a substitute for the minimum education/experience requirement: Bachelor’s Degree and seven (7) years of related experience; OR Doctoral Degree and three (3) years of related experience Knowledge of student development in higher education. Experience leading students in developing a sense of purpose and developing education and personal goals. Proficiency using word processing, spreadsheet, database applications, student information systems, degree planning, and degree audits. Ability to multitask and work cooperatively with others, present information clearly and concisely, work with sensitive information and maintain confidentiality. Excellent written communication, analytical, interpersonal, and organizational skills. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience with student success technology platforms. Banner, Degree Works, Navigate 360, Argos, Laserfiche. Experience working with First Year, Transfer, and Graduate students. Experience supervising professional staff. SALARY: Up to $4,544.67 Monthly ($54,536.04 Annual, Approximately) NOTE:  Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. BENEFITS (rules, policies, eligibility apply) From our generous benefits package and professional development opportunities, to our retirement programs and our commitment to service excellence, the Island University is an engaging and rewarding place to work. INSURANCE: Medical $0 - $30 per month for Employee Only coverage after university contribution ($920 value). Up to 83% of premium covered by the university: Employee and Spouse Employee and Children Employee and Family coverage Dental & Vision Life Insurance, Accidental D&D, Long Term Disability, Flexible Spending Account and Day Care Spending Account DUAL CAREER PARTNER PROGRAM ON-CAMPUS WELLNESS OPPORTUNITIES Work Life Solutions: Counseling, Work Life Assistance, Financial Resources, and Legal Resources Tuition Benefits: Public Loan Forgiveness Book scholarships 100% tuition coverage for up to 18 credit hours per fiscal year (other rules may apply). Retirement : Teacher Retirement System of Texas (TRS) or Optional Retirement Plan (ORP) Voluntary Tax Deferred Account/Deferred Compensation Plan Time Off : 8+ hours of vacation paid time off every month. 8 hours of sick leave time off every month. 8 hours of paid time off for Birthday leave. 12-15 paid holidays each year. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Scheduling-Specialist_R-096701-1": {
      "title": "Scheduling Specialist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Title Scheduling Specialist Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department Office Of The Registrar Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Staff Job Description TAMU-CC is a dynamic university designated as both a Hispanic-Serving Institution (HSI) and Minority-Serving Institution (MSI) with approximately 11,000 students from 47 states and 54 foreign nations. We employ over 1,400 full-time and 2,000 part-time Islanders (including students/GAs). The University attracts highly talented faculty and staff and offers an array of undergraduate and graduate degrees, including doctoral programs.  As a member of the Texas A&M University System, TAMU-CC benefits from a range of resources, increased visibility and influence, and opportunities to collaborate in mutually beneficial ways with peers across member institutions and associated agencies.   TAMU-CC’s beautiful campus is located on a 240-acre island on Corpus Christi Bay and was ranked #1 College by the Sea by Best College Reviews. Our natural setting is enhanced by its modern, attractive, and state-of-the-art classroom buildings and support facilities. PURPOSE The Scheduling Specialist, under general supervision, is responsible for updating and maintaining university files. This position assists in developing and updating records, as well as departmental and university policies and procedures. The Scheduling Specialist answers a multi-line telephone, troubleshooting issues presented by callers, assists with handling student record issues, and provides optimal customer service.  This role will be part of the university’s Centralized Scheduling team by working with academic units to build the course schedule and assist with scheduling classrooms, computer labs, and events in academic spaces.  Extensive interaction with the public, faculty, staff, students, parents, and visitors is required. DESCRIPTION Functional Area 1: Operations Percent Effort: 45% Create an effective and efficient student-centered, service-oriented environment by providing excellent customer service, including but not limited to answering telephones promptly and processing students' record changes accurately and timely. Answer multi-line telephones and troubleshoot any issues presented by callers. Audit and award undergraduate degrees. Assist with printing, preparation, and mail-out of diplomas. Ensure compliance with academic and regulatory requirements, including adherence to FERPA guidelines, demonstrating accuracy, timeliness, and professionalism. Aid with registration, transcripts, enrollment certifications and verifications, and end of term processes. Process student registration adds, drops, and withdrawals from the University. Functional Area 2: Program Management Percent Effort: 35% Deploy and maintain ongoing efforts for optimized, strategic scheduling of academic courses and ad hoc meetings in classrooms, class labs, and computer labs utilizing Ad Astra scheduling Software. Assist in centralized course schedule building and be a point of contact of Academic colleges for trouble shooting during course building process. Assist Assistant Registrar with course schedule building while working with academic units to ensure compliance. Functional Area 3:  Administrative Support Percent Effort: 20% Assist with Commencement and registrar-related events throughout the year. Assist Assistant Registrar with special projects and perform other duties as assigned. QUALIFICATIONS Associate’s degree in applicable field. Three (3) years of related experience. Additional experience may be used as a substitute for the minimum education requirement: High School Diploma or GED and five (5) years of related experience Intermediate skills in Microsoft Office Suite (Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Outlook, Teams) within a Windows environment. Excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills to be able to communicate effectively in a positive, courteous, professional manner with various populations of customers in person, telephone, or email. Self-motivated, self-directed, and has excellent attention to detail skills. Ability to work outside regular business hours and weekends as needed for commencement and university programs. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor’s degree in Communication, Business, or related field. Experience in academic scheduling, records, or higher education administrative support. Experience with Banner Student Information System, Ad Astra software, Laserfiche, and Argos. Customer service experience in a higher education environment. Familiarity with FERPA and student privacy standards. SALARY:  U p to $17.31 Hourly (Up to $36,004.80 Annually, Approximately) NOTE:   Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. BENEFITS (rules, policies, eligibility apply) From our generous benefits package and professional development opportunities, to our retirement programs and our commitment to service excellence, the Island University is an engaging and rewarding place to work. INSURANCE: Medical $0 - $30 per month for Employee Only coverage after university contribution ($920 value). Up to 83% of premium covered by the university: Employee and Spouse Employee and Children Employee and Family coverage Dental & Vision Life Insurance, Accidental D&D, Long Term Disability, Flexible Spending Account and Day Care Spending Account DUAL CAREER PARTNER PROGRAM ON-CAMPUS WELLNESS OPPORTUNITIES Work Life Solutions: Counseling, Work Life Assistance, Financial Resources, and Legal Resources Tuition Benefits: Public Loan Forgiveness Book scholarships 100% tuition coverage for up to 18 credit hours per fiscal year (other rules may apply). Retirement : Teacher Retirement System of Texas (TRS) or Optional Retirement Plan (ORP) Voluntary Tax Deferred Account/Deferred Compensation Plan Time Off : 8+ hours of vacation paid time off every month. 8 hours of sick leave time off every month. 8 hours of paid time off for Birthday leave. 12-15 paid holidays each year. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Software-Applications-Developer-I_R-096789": {
      "title": "Software Applications Developer I",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Title Software Applications Developer I Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department Harte Research Institute Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Staff Job Description TAMU-CC is a dynamic university designated as both a Hispanic-Serving Institution (HSI) and Minority-Serving Institution (MSI) with approximately 11,000 students from 47 states and 54 foreign nations. We employ over 1,400 full-time and 2,000 part-time Islanders (including students/GAs). The University attracts highly talented faculty and staff and offers an array of undergraduate and graduate degrees, including doctoral programs.  As a member of the Texas A&M University System, TAMU-CC benefits from a range of resources, increased visibility and influence, and opportunities to collaborate in mutually beneficial ways with peers across member institutions and associated agencies.   TAMU-CC’s beautiful campus is located on a 240-acre island on Corpus Christi Bay and was ranked #1 College by the Sea by Best College Reviews. Our natural setting is enhanced by its modern, attractive, and state-of-the-art classroom buildings and support facilities. PURPOSE The Software Applications Developer I, under direction, develops and maintains code and workflows that support ecological modeling and analysis, working closely with scientists and end-users to ensure code quality, usability, and reproducibility.   DESCRIPTION Functional Area 1: Modeling Application Development Percent Effort: 60% Develops R code in support of ecological modeling, including scripts and functions used to run models. Analyzes biological, environmental, and related datasets to prepare inputs and evaluate model outputs. Utilizes and creates NetCDF files for data inputs and model outputs. Develops and maintains R packages. Functional Area 2: Code Development and Management Percent Effort: 25% Develops and maintains Git repositories for ecological modeling and data analysis. Develops interactive data applications for users to visualize and interact with input data and model outputs. Develops and maintains documentation for code and workflows to support reproducible use of modeling tools. Assists in development of lab policies relating to code development, sharing, and version control.   Functional Area 3: User Engagement and Documentation Percent Effort: 15% Collaborates with software users (stakeholders, scientists, students, partners) throughout the development process, incorporating feedback into design and implementation of modeling tools. Provides support to users on running models and accessing data products. Documents workflows to promote reproducible science. QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor’s degree in an applicable field. Four months of related experience. Knowledge of word processing and spreadsheet applications. Knowledge of intermediate troubleshooting, client relations, formulating and contributing ideas. Ability to multitask and work cooperatively with others. Ability to maintain the security and integrity of critical infrastructure, which may include communications systems, computer networks and systems, cybersecurity systems, electrical grid, hazardous waste treatment system or water treatment system.   PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience developing code for ecological modeling. Experience working with spatial ecological data, including use of R (e.g., terra) and ArcGIS to prepare inputs for ecological models. Experience collaborating with software users throughout the development process, incorporating feedback into design and implementation. Fluency with Git-based version control and collaborative development workflows, including use of CI pipelines. Experience creating NetCDF files that conform to current CF metadata conventions. Proficiency in R and Python for data analysis, visualization, and ecological model development. Experience developing and maintaining R packages to support analytical workflows. Experience developing interactive data applications. (e.g., R Shiny) NOTE:  Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. This position is grant-funded. Continued employment is contingent upon continued funding. BENEFITS (rules, policies, eligibility apply) From our generous benefits package and professional development opportunities, to our retirement programs and our commitment to service excellence, the Island University is an engaging and rewarding place to work. INSURANCE: Medical $0 - $30 per month for Employee Only coverage after university contribution ($920 value). Up to 83% of premium covered by the university: Employee and Spouse Employee and Children Employee and Family coverage Dental & Vision Life Insurance, Accidental D&D, Long Term Disability, Flexible Spending Account and Day Care Spending Account DUAL CAREER PARTNER PROGRAM ON-CAMPUS WELLNESS OPPORTUNITIES Work Life Solutions: Counseling, Work Life Assistance, Financial Resources, and Legal Resources Tuition Benefits: Public Loan Forgiveness Book scholarships 100% tuition coverage for up to 18 credit hours per fiscal year (other rules may apply). Retirement : Teacher Retirement System of Texas (TRS) or Optional Retirement Plan (ORP) Voluntary Tax Deferred Account/Deferred Compensation Plan Time Off : 8+ hours of vacation paid time off every month. 8 hours of sick leave time off every month. 8 hours of paid time off for Birthday leave. 12-15 paid holidays each year. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Associate-Director--University-Center_R-096588-1": {
      "title": "Associate Director, University Center",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Title Associate Director, University Center Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department Student Life Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Staff Job Description TAMU-CC is a dynamic university designated as both a Hispanic-Serving Institution (HSI) and Minority-Serving Institution (MSI) with approximately 11,000 students from 47 states and 54 foreign nations. We employ over 1,400 full-time and 2,000 part-time Islanders (including students/GAs). The University attracts highly talented faculty and staff and offers an array of undergraduate and graduate degrees, including doctoral programs.  As a member of the Texas A&M University System, TAMU-CC benefits from a range of resources, increased visibility and influence, and opportunities to collaborate in mutually beneficial ways with peers across member institutions and associated agencies.   TAMU-CC’s beautiful campus is located on a 240-acre island on Corpus Christi Bay and was ranked #1 College by the Sea by Best College Reviews. Our natural setting is enhanced by its modern, attractive, and state-of-the-art classroom buildings and support facilities. PURPOSE The Associate Director is primarily responsible for all aspects of departmental operations, project management, supervising professional and para-professional staff and maintains responsibility for the reservations department. The Associate Director must be able to function independently and professionally in the assigned areas of responsibility and will serve as the acting director in the absence of the director. The Associate Director advises the Director in all aspects of departmental operations and project management, and maintains responsibility for the reservations department. This position will supervise event staff, graduate assistant(s) and student employees. DESCRIPTION Functional Area 1: Reservations and Information Desk Percent Effort:  40% Provide leadership for the reservation functions of the UC, including interpretation, application, and enforcement of relevant policies and procedures. Provide regular event planning support and training to internal clients. Oversee the performance of complex scheduling based on a tiered reservations system, including prioritizing the needs of clients, and recommending alternative courses of action when needed; contribute to and participate in long-range event planning for high-profile and traditional campus events; demonstrate good judgment in making decisions and managing reservable spaces. Communicate with clients to ensure smooth-running, effective events, including leading the successful resolution of potential and real problems in a timely manner using tact, sensitivity, discretion, and political acumen. Communicate with service partners (health and safety, IT, custodial, facilities, parking, etc.) and co-workers to coordinate services and staffing needs for events. Lead the Event Services Planning Network (ESPN) to coordinate with and support event planners across campus. Ensure quality service, programmatic success, participant safety and facility security, and cleanliness. Respond to and report any safety and security concerns following established protocols. Serve as campus lead for Mazevo, reservation software, supporting other facility managers utilizing Mazevo and working with IT on software updates and related needs. Functional Area 2: Administration Percent Effort:  40% Lead staff team to develop and implement student staff training for all UC student employees.  Update and maintain the UC student employee handbook and the Reservations/Scheduling handbook.   Provides leadership for department along with and in absence of the Director. Serves on the management team with Director. Represents the department, division, or University on various committees. Participates in developing departmental strategic plans based on department goals. Plans budget for department(s) under their supervision and assists Director in planning and budgeting (including setting annual revenue targets) for department. Ensure Mazevo/event billing is accurate and completed assisting coordinators. Review and verify accuracy of monthly billing and other reports; investigate and correct errors. Participates in planning, directing, and coordinating operational or procedural matters to meet department goals and objectives. Develops, implements, coordinates, and evaluates programs. Monitors compliance with all unit, department, University, and government standards, policies, and procedures. Develops reports, analyses, and surveys as requested by Director. Handles special projects assigned by the Director. Review and verify the accuracy of reports; investigate and correct errors. Oversee the process of Interviewing, hiring, training, and evaluating approximately 10 to 16 student employees. Clearly define performance expectations, ensure accountability, and provide ongoing informal feedback, coaching and mentoring along with and in the absence of the Events Coordinators. Serve as a team leader on the UC staff, actively developing and implementing strategic plans, goal setting, and attainment; serve as a key player for emergency preparations and evacuations. Articulate the UC mission and goals to clients as it relates to building usage. Perform other duties as assigned. Functional Area 3: Marketing and Public Relations Percent Effort:  20% Cultivate the department’s online identity through the department website and social media accounts; promote the UC as a department and the resources, services, and activities available in the UC Advise and lead the University Center Programs (UCP) student employees to develop a strategic calendar of promotional, passive, and traditional events to create a sense of belonging among students. QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor’s degree. Eight (8) years of progressively responsible experience in a student union/center. Additional education may be considered as a substitute for the minimum experience requirement: Master’s degree in higher education, communication, leadership, or related field and six (6) years of progressively responsible experience in a student union/center. Doctoral degree in higher education, communication, leadership, or related field and four (4) years of progressively responsible experience in a student union/center. Intermediate skills in Microsoft Office Suite. Advanced skills as an administrator in Mazevo or EMS (event management software), and room diagramming software, such as Prismm, Allseated or Social Tables. Capacity to multitask complex assignments in a fast-paced environment. Strong interpersonal, organizational, written, and verbal communication skills to be able to establish and maintain courteous, effective working relationships with students, faculty, and staff. Skilled in resolving conflicts and ability to successfully navigate challenging conversations.    Experience supervising and training student employees. Demonstrated experience providing excellent customer service to a variety of clients. Ability to work a flexible schedule, including some evenings/weekends scheduled in advance and on short notice.  PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Master’s degree in higher education, communication, leadership, or related field. Experience supervising full-time, professional employees. Experience designing and implementing leadership training for professional staff, student leaders and or student employees. Experience coordinating or managing events for a facility that hosts high visibility, high impact, and high-risk events. SALARY: up to $65,000/annually, approximately up to $5,416.67/monthly NOTE: Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. BENEFITS (rules, policies, eligibility apply) From our generous benefits package and professional development opportunities, to our retirement programs and our commitment to service excellence, the Island University is an engaging and rewarding place to work. Insurance Medical $0 - $30 per month for Employee Only coverage after university contribution ($920 value). Up to 83% of premium covered by the university: Employee and Spouse Employee and Children Employee and Family coverage Dental & Vision Life Insurance, Accidental D&D, Long Term Disability, Flexible Spending Account and Day Care Spending Account On-campus wellness opportunities Work Life Solutions: Counseling, Work Life Assistance, Financial Resources, and Legal Resources Tuition Benefits: Public Loan Forgiveness Book scholarships 100% tuition coverage for up to 18 credit hours per fiscal year (other rules may apply). Retirement Teacher Retirement System of Texas (TRS) or Optional Retirement Plan (ORP) Voluntary Tax Deferred Account/Deferred Compensation Plan Time Off: 8+ hours of vacation paid time off every month. 8 hours of sick leave time off every month. 8 hours of paid time off for Birthday leave. 12-15 paid holidays each year. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Temporary-Support-Pool_R-096766-1": {
      "title": "Temporary Support Pool",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJob Title Temporary Support Pool Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department Human Resources Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Temporary/Casual Staff (Fixed Term) Job Description RESPONSIBILITIES Provide support for departmental, administrative, project-based, or research-related activities as assigned which could include but are not limited to: Perform a variety of clerical, administrative, operational, and customer service functions. Assist with data entry, record keeping, document preparation, and information management. Support special projects, research initiatives, event coordination, or other assigned tasks. Communicate professionally with students, employees, vendors, and the public in person, by phone, or electronically as needed. Maintain files, reports, databases, and other records as needed. Utilize office technology and software applications to complete assigned work. Perform other duties as assigned based on departmental needs. QUALIFICATIONS High School Diploma or GED. Experience using computers and standard office equipment. Strong organizational, communication, and customer service skills. Ability to manage multiple tasks, meet deadlines, and work independently or as part of a team. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience with Microsoft Office applications, including Word, Excel, Outlook, and PowerPoint. College coursework or specialized training in Business, Communications, Information Technology, Research, or a related field. Previous experience providing administrative, project, research, clerical, or customer service support. Experience working with databases, records management systems, or enterprise software applications. Ability to quickly learn new software systems, processes, and procedures. NOTE:   This posting is for temporary assignments and employment opportunities that arise on an as-needed basis. Duties, schedules, work locations, and assignment lengths may vary depending on departmental needs. Please email employment@tamucc.edu  if you have any questions regarding your application submitted for this position or regarding this posting. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Police-Officer_R-093790-1": {
      "title": "Police Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 8 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Title Police Officer Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department University Police Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Staff Job Description TAMU-CC is a dynamic university designated as both a Hispanic-Serving Institution (HSI) and Minority-Serving Institution (MSI) with approximately 11,000 students from 47 states and 54 foreign nations. We employ over 1,400 full-time and 2,000 part-time Islanders (including students/GAs). The University attracts highly talented faculty and staff and offers an array of undergraduate and graduate degrees, including doctoral programs.  As a member of the Texas A&M University System, TAMU-CC benefits from a range of resources, increased visibility and influence, and opportunities to collaborate in mutually beneficial ways with peers across member institutions and associated agencies.   TAMU-CC’s beautiful campus is located on a 240-acre island on Corpus Christi Bay and was ranked #1 College by the Sea by Best College Reviews. Our natural setting is enhanced by its modern, attractive, and state-of-the-art classroom buildings and support facilities.   PURPOSE This position is responsible for providing law enforcement duties for the campus community. Responds to all emergencies and calls for assistance. DESCRIPTION Functional Area 1:  Enforcement Percent Effort: 50 Respond to calls of police service involving a wide variety of activity. Document all incidents both criminal and non-criminal utilizing CLERK (Computerized Law Enforcement Record Keeping) system. Enforce criminal and traffic laws as well as university rules and regulations. Arrest, cite, or refer persons as appropriate when in violation. Testify in court for criminal and traffic offenses. Functional Area 2:  Patrol Percent Effort: 40 Patrol property both on and off campus that is owned or controlled by TAMU-CC, by foot, bike, cart, or motor vehicle. Provide security during special details. Provide security escorts when requested. Functional Area 3:  Other Percent Effort: 10 Unlock doors and buildings. Assist the campus community with vehicle jumpstarts and unlocks. Assist other divisions within the University Police Department as needed. Perform other duties as assigned. QUALIFICATIONS Police Officer I: High School Diploma or GED. One (1) year of related experience in law enforcement. Additional education may be considered as a substitution for the minimum experience requirement. Basic peace officer certification through TCOLE. Police Officer II: High School Diploma or GED. One (1) year of related experience in law enforcement. Additional education may be considered as a substitution for the minimum experience requirement. Intermediate peace officer certification through TCOLE. Police Officer III: High School Diploma or GED. Three (3) years of related experience in law enforcement. Additional education may be considered as a substitution for the minimum experience requirement. Advanced peace officer certification through TCOLE. Police Officer IV: High School Diploma or GED. Five (5) years of related experience in law enforcement. Additional education may be considered as a substitution for the minimum experience requirement. Master peace officer certification through TCOLE. ADDITIONAL SKILLS/QUALIFICATIONS Ability to work shift work. Ability to pass a firearms qualification. Ability to multitask and work cooperatively with others. Ability to communicate effectively with all segments of the campus population. Ability to function under adverse and stressful conditions. Maintain a valid Driver License. PERKS Tuition reimbursement available after three (3) months of employment. Wellness reimbursement programs. Discounts to local area businesses and cell phone companies. NOTE: Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. BENEFITS (rules, policies, eligibility apply) From our generous benefits package and professional development opportunities, to our retirement programs and our commitment to service excellence, the Island University is an engaging and rewarding place to work. Insurance Medical $0 - $30 per month for Employee Only coverage after university contribution ($920 value). Up to 83% of premium covered by the university: Employee and Spouse Employee and Children Employee and Family coverage Dental & Vision Life Insurance, Accidental D&D, Long Term Disability, Flexible Spending Account and Day Care Spending Account On-campus wellness opportunities Work Life Solutions: Counseling, Work Life Assistance, Financial Resources, and Legal Resources Tuition Benefits: Public Loan Forgiveness Book scholarships 100% tuition coverage for up to 18 credit hours per fiscal year (other rules may apply). Retirement Teacher Retirement System of Texas (TRS) or Optional Retirement Plan (ORP) Voluntary Tax Deferred Account/Deferred Compensation Plan Time Off: 8+ hours of vacation paid time off every month. 8 hours of sick leave time off every month. 8 hours of paid time off for Birthday leave. 12-15 paid holidays each year. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Professional-Counselor-I-II--University-Counseling-Center_R-096658-1": {
      "title": "Professional Counselor I-II, University Counseling Center",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-31\nPosted: Posted 9 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Title Professional Counselor I-II, University Counseling Center Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department University Counseling Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Staff Job Description TAMU-CC is a dynamic university designated as both a Hispanic-Serving Institution (HSI) and Minority-Serving Institution (MSI) with approximately 11,000 students from 47 states and 54 foreign nations. We employ over 1,400 full-time and 2,000 part-time Islanders (including students/GAs). The University attracts highly talented faculty and staff and offers an array of undergraduate and graduate degrees, including doctoral programs.  As a member of the Texas A&M University System, TAMU-CC benefits from a range of resources, increased visibility and influence, and opportunities to collaborate in mutually beneficial ways with peers across member institutions and associated agencies. TAMU-CC’s beautiful campus is located on a 240-acre island on Corpus Christi Bay and was ranked #1 College by the Sea by Best College Reviews. Our natural setting is enhanced by its modern, attractive, and state-of-the-art classroom buildings and support facilities. PURPOSE The Professional Counselor I/II serves the Texas A&M University-Corpus Christi community by providing a full continuum of psychological services with a specialized focus on crisis intervention, triage assessment, and alcohol and other drug (AOD) screening and programming. Under general supervision, this position delivers individual and group counseling, comprehensive clinical/triage assessments, clinical supervision, and outreach/consultation.    RESPONSIBILITIES Functional Area 1: Clinical Services Percent Effort: 25%         Provide brief individual counseling, consultation, and referrals. Conduct alcohol and drug screenings and comprehensive substance use assessments. Facilitate recovery-focused group sessions, psychoeducational classes, and support groups, as assigned. Maintain accurate/timely documentation in the electronic medical records system. Participate in biweekly case conferences and informal case consultations for client care coordination. Functional Area 2: Crisis/Triage Consultation                                       Percent Effort: 40%         Conduct triage and crisis assessments including de-escalation, safety/risk evaluation, and stabilization planning during daily crisis walk-in hours. Coordinate referrals to a higher level of care (inpatient hospitalization, intensive outpatient, etc.) as needed. Follow up with students in crisis to monitor progress and ensure linkage to support. Functional Area 3: Consultation & Outreach       Percent Effort: 15%         Develop and deliver presentations, workshops, and outreach programs related to mental health, suicide prevention, and AOD issues.     Engage in campus-wide prevention and early intervention efforts. Serve as liaison and consultant to campus and community partners, including collegiate recovery/programming initiatives. Provide short-term embedded support services in conjunction with campus programs including occasional weekend and evening programs (e.g., annual university camp for freshman). Functional Area 4: Program Coordination          Percent Effort: 10%         Coordinate a specialized program area (e.g., AOD, recovery programming, embedded support for specific student population). Collaborate with campus partners to optimize care coordination and referral pathways. Functional Area 5: Clinical Supervision  Percent Effort: 10%         Provide clinical supervision to master’s level counseling practicum trainees and interns. Participate in selection, onboarding, and training of counseling practicum trainees and interns. QUALIFICATIONS Professional Counselor I:  Master’s degree in Counseling, Clinical Psychology, Social Work or other specialty related to job function. Practical/field placement relevant to college counseling, or supervised experience related to job function. Licensed as Licensed Professional Counselor Associate.   Must maintain licensure for continued employment. Professional Counselor II:  Master’s degree in counseling, Clinical Psychology, Social Work or other specialty related to job function. Texas Licensed Professional Counselor (LPC), Licensed Marriage and Family Therapist (LMFT), or Licensed Clinical Social Worker (LCSW). Must maintain licensure for continued employment. LPC’s must have completed at least 3000 hours of post-licensure clinical mental health experience in no less than 18 months of supervision. LMFT’s must have completed 3000 clock hours of full-time post-licensure clinical experience in no less than 24 months with 200 hours of supervision. LCSW’s must have completed at least one (1) year of supervised clinical experience with 100 hours of supervision and have passed their state board exam. OTHER REQUIRED SKILLS AND ABILITIES Demonstrated experience in brief therapy and/or solution focused therapy with adults in an outpatient setting. Demonstrated experience assessing and treating a wide variety of clinical conditions. Demonstrated experience in crisis intervention.    Strong interpersonal communication skills (verbal and written) to be able to effectively collaborate within a multidisciplinary environment and the campus community.  Knowledge and experience with Electronic Medical Records systems Knowledge and experience with telemental health platforms and providing telemental health services. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Licensed as Licensed Professional Counselor (LPC) in the state of Texas or able to obtain this licensure in Texas within six months of hire if licensed in another state. Two (2) or more years of post-graduate experience in social work, counseling, or other mental health agency. Experience providing counseling services as a professional staff member in a university setting. Experience in assessment and intervention of individuals with serious mental health concerns and/or a history of trauma.   Demonstrated program development and coordination skills. Advanced clinical training and experience in specialized area (e.g., trauma, eating disorders, OCD, AOD) Advanced clinical training, experience and certification in an evidence-based therapy modality (e.g., EMDR, DBT, ACT, CBT).   Experience providing clinical supervision to master’s level counseling and psychology trainees utilizing a multiculturally competent approach. SALARY: $49,000 – $60,000/annually, approximately $4,083.34 - $5,000/monthly Salary is commensurate with experience and qualifications. NOTE: Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. BENEFITS (rules, policies, eligibility apply) From our generous benefits package and professional development opportunities, to our retirement programs and our commitment to service excellence, the Island University is an engaging and rewarding place to work. Insurance Medical $0 - $30 per month for Employee Only coverage after university contribution ($920 value). Up to 83% of premium covered by the university: Employee and Spouse Employee and Children Employee and Family coverage Dental & Vision Life Insurance, Accidental D&D, Long Term Disability, Flexible Spending Account and Day Care Spending Account On-campus wellness opportunities Work Life Solutions: Counseling, Work Life Assistance, Financial Resources, and Legal Resources Tuition Benefits: Public Loan Forgiveness Book scholarships 100% tuition coverage for up to 18 credit hours per fiscal year (other rules may apply). Retirement Teacher Retirement System of Texas (TRS) or Optional Retirement Plan (ORP) Voluntary Tax Deferred Account/Deferred Compensation Plan Time Off: 8+ hours of vacation paid time off every month. 8 hours of sick leave time off every month. 8 hours of paid time off for Birthday leave. 12-15 paid holidays each year. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Coordinator-of-Outdoor-Recreation---Student-Development_R-096622-1": {
      "title": "Coordinator of Outdoor Recreation & Student Development",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-27\nPosted: Posted 13 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Title Coordinator of Outdoor Recreation & Student Development Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department Recreational Sports Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Staff Job Description TAMU-CC is a dynamic university designated as both a Hispanic-Serving Institution (HSI) and Minority-Serving Institution (MSI) with approximately 11,000 students from 47 states and 54 foreign nations. We employ over 1,400 full-time and 2,000 part-time Islanders (including students/GAs). The University attracts highly talented faculty and staff and offers an array of undergraduate and graduate degrees, including doctoral programs.  As a member of the Texas A&M University System, TAMU-CC benefits from a range of resources, increased visibility and influence, and opportunities to collaborate in mutually beneficial ways with peers across member institutions and associated agencies.   TAMU-CC’s beautiful campus is located on a 240-acre island on Corpus Christi Bay and was ranked #1 College by the Sea by Best College Reviews. Our natural setting is enhanced by its modern, attractive, and state-of-the-art classroom buildings and support facilities. PURPOSE The Coordinator of Outdoor Recreation & Student Development provides leadership for the Outdoor Recreation program while overseeing department-wide student employee development initiatives. This position develops outdoor adventure experiences, risk management practices, leadership programs, and student employment training that foster student engagement, retention, and success. The coordinator collaborates across the department to ensure high-quality experiential learning opportunities that align with the mission of Recreational Sports and Texas A&M University-Corpus Christi.   DESCRIPTION Functional Area 1: Outdoor Recreation Programs & Operations Percent Effort: 60% Implement, lead, and evaluate comprehensive Outdoor Recreation programs and experiences for students, faculty, and staff utilizing university resources, as well as local, state, and national parks and natural areas. Develop and coordinate outdoor adventure programming including trips, workshops, clinics, team-building experiences, and special events that promote physical wellness, mental growth, environmental awareness, and student engagement. Oversee the Outdoor Recreation equipment rental program including inventory management, equipment maintenance, purchasing, replacement planning, and daily operations. Recruit, hire, train, schedule, supervise, and evaluate trip leaders, rental equipment staff, future rock wall staff, and student employees. Assist in the maintenance and event planning of the Hike & Bike Trail in conjunction with the facilities unit.   Manage and maintain the rock wall facility, ensuring equipment inspections, cleanliness, operational standards, and a safe environment for participants. Oversee risk management practices for all Outdoor Recreation program areas including the equipment garage, rock wall operations, trips, transportation, and participant safety procedures. Develop and maintain emergency action plans, safety procedures, participant documentation, and operational protocols consistent with university policies and industry standards. Maintain a safe environment while encouraging participants to challenge themselves through new experiences and experiential learning opportunities. Oversee the development, implementation, and daily operations of future Beachfront Recreation programming, including program planning, safety procedures, equipment needs, staffing models, participant experiences, and operational standards. Safely operates university vehicles. Functional Area 2: Student Development & Leadership Percent Effort: 20% Develop and facilitate student employee leadership development opportunities focused on technical skills, professional growth, customer service, and risk management. Conduct regular student staff training, in-services, workshops, and skill development opportunities for Outdoor Recreation employees. Provide mentorship, supervision, and professional development opportunities for student employees and assigned Student Program Assistant(s). Facilitate team-building programs and experiential learning activities for students, faculty, and staff across the university. Create intentional learning opportunities that support student growth, leadership development, confidence, teamwork, and personal responsibility. Evaluate student employee performance and provide ongoing feedback, coaching, and development opportunities. Support departmental efforts to enhance student engagement, belonging, and success through recreational experiences. Functional Area 3: Department Administration, Assessment & Collaboration Percent Effort: 20% Serve as one of the department's certified First Aid/CPR/AED Instructors by conducting certification courses and maintaining training records for student employees and staff. Manage Outdoor Recreation budgets including trip expenses, special events, equipment inventory, student wages, and operational expenditures. Collaborate with Recreational Sports staff and campus partners to plan and execute special events, collaborative programs, and student engagement initiatives. Serve as a liaison between Texas Parks and Wildlife Department and university departments to develop outdoor programming opportunities, including service-learning and community engagement projects. Develop outdoor activity-themed collaborative programming with campus departments, student organizations, and university programs. Assist with departmental assessment efforts by collecting participation data, evaluating program effectiveness, and preparing reports related to learning outcomes and student engagement. Oversee assigned departmental special events and projects. Assist with departmental strategic initiatives, risk management efforts, and operational planning. Perform other duties as assigned. QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor's degree in Recreation, Outdoor Recreation, Parks and Recreation, Higher Education, Student Affairs, Sports Management, or a related field. One (1) year of professional experience in outdoor recreation, campus recreation, experiential education, or a related field. Experience planning, implementing, and evaluating outdoor recreation programs and special events. Experience recruiting, training, supervising, and evaluating student employees. Experience with outdoor recreation risk management, equipment operations, and participant safety. Current First Aid/CPR/AED certification or the ability to obtain certification within six (6) months of employment. Ability to obtain American Red Cross (or equivalent) First Aid/CPR/AED Instructor certification within one (1) year of employment. Valid Texas driver's license or ability to obtain within six (6) months of employment. Ability to communicate effectively, manage multiple priorities, and work collaboratively with various populations. Wilderness First Aid (WFA), Wilderness First Responder (WFR), Leave No Trace Trainer, or other nationally recognized outdoor certifications. Work some evenings and weekends, and to include possibly some overnight travel. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Master’s degree in Recreation, Higher Education, Student Affairs, Outdoor Recreation, or a related field. Experience managing a collegiate outdoor recreation program, rock wall, equipment rental center, or adventure-based programming. Current American Red Cross (or equivalent) First Aid/CPR/AED Instructor certification. Experience supervising graduate assistants and full-time or student staff. Experience developing and managing program budgets and assessing program outcomes. Experience collaborating with state agencies, parks departments, or community partners. Active involvement with NIRSA, AORE, or other related professional organizations. SALARY: $43,888 - $48,853.08/annually, approximately $3,657.34 - $4,071.09/monthly NOTE: Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. BENEFITS (rules, policies, eligibility apply) From our generous benefits package and professional development opportunities, to our retirement programs and our commitment to service excellence, the Island University is an engaging and rewarding place to work. Insurance Medical $0 - $30 per month for Employee Only coverage after university contribution ($920 value). Up to 83% of premium covered by the university: Employee and Spouse Employee and Children Employee and Family coverage Dental & Vision Life Insurance, Accidental D&D, Long Term Disability, Flexible Spending Account and Day Care Spending Account On-campus wellness opportunities Work Life Solutions: Counseling, Work Life Assistance, Financial Resources, and Legal Resources Tuition Benefits: Public Loan Forgiveness Book scholarships 100% tuition coverage for up to 18 credit hours per fiscal year (other rules may apply). Retirement Teacher Retirement System of Texas (TRS) or Optional Retirement Plan (ORP) Voluntary Tax Deferred Account/Deferred Compensation Plan Time Off: 8+ hours of vacation paid time off every month. 8 hours of sick leave time off every month. 8 hours of paid time off for Birthday leave. 12-15 paid holidays each year. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Assistant-Athletic-Director-for-Media-Relations_R-096605-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Athletic Director for Media Relations",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-27\nPosted: Posted 13 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Title Assistant Athletic Director for Media Relations Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department Athletic Programs Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Staff Job Description TAMU-CC is a dynamic university designated as both a Hispanic-Serving Institution (HSI) and Minority-Serving Institution (MSI) with approximately 11,000 students from 47 states and 54 foreign nations. We employ over 1,400 full-time and 2,000 part-time Islanders (including students/GAs). The University attracts highly talented faculty and staff and offers an array of undergraduate and graduate degrees, including doctoral programs.  As a member of the Texas A&M University System, TAMU-CC benefits from a range of resources, increased visibility and influence, and opportunities to collaborate in mutually beneficial ways with peers across member institutions and associated agencies.   TAMU-CC’s beautiful campus is located on a 240-acre island on Corpus Christi Bay and was ranked #1 College by the Sea by Best College Reviews. Our natural setting is enhanced by its modern, attractive, and state-of-the-art classroom buildings and support facilities. PURPOSE The Assistant Athletic Director for Media Relations and is responsible for all public- facing external communications, media relations, and information in support of Texas A&M University-Corpus Christi Athletics. DESCRIPTION Functional Area 1: Sports Information Percent Effort: 70% Coordinate and direct the activities of the athletic media relations staff serving 16 intercollegiate sports. Serve as the primary athletics department liaison to local, regional, and national media as well as other universities, conferences, and organizations. Serve as spokesman/ambassador for the athletics department and the university. Supervise the writing and circulating of news releases to the media. Assist with the coordination of game day operations for several sports. Maintain current and historical statistics including records for athletic teams. Nominate student-athletes for athletic and academic awards within the university, conference and NCAA. Recruit, train, supervise and schedule student-workers to help with gameday functions. Collaborate with the School of Communications on projects and internships. Responsible for live stat production at home events. Travel with assigned teams as necessary. Functional Area 2: Strategic Communication Percent Effort: 20% Manage athletics website in conjunction with the Associate AD of Marketing, Fan engagement and Creative Content (goislanders.com) with oversight of all information and content including feature stories and student-athlete spotlights. Work closely and collaboratively with external relations to a) enhance the visibility of the Islanders Athletic program in the conference, state and nationally, b) advance TAMU-CC mission, vision and branding, c) develop and implement printed and online materials aligned with Athletics design standards and TAMU-CC Brand and Style Guide. Serve as primary live broadcast web streaming technician with ESPN+ streaming productions. Maintain the infrastructure and processes behind ESPN+ streaming productions. Organize all athletics photographs and supervise archiving of all video elements. Functional Area 3: Compliance Percent Effort: 10% Administer budget for the athletic communications unit. Responsible for verifying all public facing data. Perform other duties as assigned. QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor’s Degree in communications, journalism, sports administration, or closely  related field. Two (2) years of related experience in athletics communications or broadcasting Additional education may be considered as a substitute for the minimum experience required: Master’s degree in communications, journalism, sports administration, or closely related field Intermediate skills in Microsoft Office Suite (Word, Excel, PowerPoint, and Outlook). Excellent verbal and written communication skills. PREFFERED QUALIFICATIONS Master’s Degree. Demonstrated student-worker supervisory experience. Strong knowledge of SIDEARM CMS or comparable website management software. Experience with Adobe Premier or other video editing software. Live video streaming experience. Intermediate skills in various computer programs including StatCrew, Genius, Photoshop and InDesign. NOTE: Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. BENEFITS (rules, policies, eligibility apply) From our generous benefits package and professional development opportunities, to our retirement programs and our commitment to service excellence, the Island University is an engaging and rewarding place to work. Insurance Medical $0 - $30 per month for Employee Only coverage after university contribution ($920 value). Up to 83% of premium covered by the university: Employee and Spouse Employee and Children Employee and Family coverage Dental & Vision Life Insurance, Accidental D&D, Long Term Disability, Flexible Spending Account and Day Care Spending Account On-campus wellness opportunities Work Life Solutions: Counseling, Work Life Assistance, Financial Resources, and Legal Resources Tuition Benefits: Public Loan Forgiveness Book scholarships 100% tuition coverage for up to 18 credit hours per fiscal year (other rules may apply). Retirement Teacher Retirement System of Texas (TRS) or Optional Retirement Plan (ORP) Voluntary Tax Deferred Account/Deferred Compensation Plan Time Off: 8+ hours of vacation paid time off every month. 8 hours of sick leave time off every month. 8 hours of paid time off for Birthday leave. 12-15 paid holidays each year. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Manager--Research-Project-Administration_R-096548-1": {
      "title": "Manager, Research Project Administration",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-25\nPosted: Posted 15 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Title Manager, Research Project Administration Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department Autonomy Research Institute Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Staff Job Description TAMU-CC is a dynamic university designated as both a Hispanic-Serving Institution (HSI) and Minority-Serving Institution (MSI) with approximately 11,000 students from 47 states and 54 foreign nations. We employ over 1,400 full-time and 2,000 part-time Islanders (including students/GAs). The University attracts highly talented faculty and staff and offers an array of undergraduate and graduate degrees, including doctoral programs.  As a member of the Texas A&M University System, TAMU-CC benefits from a range of resources, increased visibility and influence, and opportunities to collaborate in mutually beneficial ways with peers across member institutions and associated agencies.   TAMU-CC’s beautiful campus is located on a 240-acre island on Corpus Christi Bay and was ranked #1 College by the Sea by Best College Reviews. Our natural setting is enhanced by its modern, attractive, and state-of-the-art classroom buildings and support facilities. PURPOSE The Manager, Research Project Administration manages and oversees the administrative, financial, and compliance activities associated with sponsored research projects in collaboration with Autonomy Research Institute (ARI) Principal Investigators (PIs) and project partners. Responsibilities include managing sponsored research administration, overseeing financial monitoring and sponsor reporting, ensuring regulatory compliance, managing sponsor communications, advising and supporting PIs, and supervising assigned sponsored project staff responsible for financial monitoring, billing, and sponsor coordination. The Manager, Research Project Administration, under the direction of the Assistant Director, Finance (ADF) or designee, manages the administrative, financial, and compliance activities associated with sponsored research projects, grants, and contracts. This position serves as ARI’s subject matter expert for sponsored research administration by ensuring compliance with sponsor requirements, managing pre-award and post-award administrative activities, advising and supporting PIs, supervising assigned sponsored project staff, and overseeing research administration processes throughout the project lifecycle. RESPONSIBILITIES Sponsored Research Administration: 50% Leads sponsored research administration activities, including monitoring expenditures, validating financial transactions, and documenting costs in accordance with approved budgets and sponsor requirements. Administers pre-award and post-award sponsored project administration activities to ensure efficient operations and full compliance with Texas A&M University System (TAMUS), state, and sponsor fiscal policies. Oversees sponsor financial and administrative reports, supporting documentation, and analyses for stakeholders, including PIs, internal departments, sponsors, and auditors. Ensures compliance with cost-sharing commitments and sponsor-specific requirements. Directs sponsored project closeout activities, ensuring all financial, administrative, and compliance requirements are completed accurately and on time. Oversees time and effort reporting and certification to ensure regulatory compliance and audit readiness. Compliance & Audits: 30% Ensures organizational compliance with sponsor, institutional, and regulatory financial requirements governing sponsored programs. Serves as ARI’s subject matter expert and primary resource for sponsored research regulations and sponsor requirements. Interprets sponsor guidelines, advises PIs and project teams regarding compliance requirements, and oversees related administrative actions. Leads audit preparation and response activities, including coordinating documentation, addressing findings, and implementing corrective actions. Develops and updates procedures related to sponsored project administration in consultation with the ADF and appropriate university offices. Manages administrative communications with sponsors and serves as the primary liaison to obtain approvals for budget revisions, compliance actions, key personnel changes, no-cost extensions, and supplemental funding requests. Reviews and interprets award terms and conditions and oversees the administrative actions necessary to ensure compliance with sponsor, institutional, and regulatory requirements. Advises ARI leadership on sponsor regulations, research administration best practices, compliance risks, and changes to federal or sponsor requirements affecting sponsored projects. Facilitates institutional approvals and sponsor prior approval requests in accordance with Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi (TAMU-CC) and sponsor requirements. Oversees the maintenance of documentation required for sponsor monitoring, audits, and regulatory reviews. Ensures the accuracy, completeness, and retention of research administration records and documentation throughout the sponsored project lifecycle. Identifies, recommends, and implements approved process improvements to enhance efficiency, compliance, and service to PIs. Program Support: 20% Collaborates with PIs and project partners and manages the alignment of financial and administrative activities with project goals and sponsor requirements. Ensures accurate and timely entry and maintenance of project data within TAMU-CC and TAMUS systems. Establishes and maintains sponsored project records and University research administration systems to ensure data accuracy, document integrity, and regulatory compliance. Administers award setup, modifications, closeout activities, and administrative documentation throughout the sponsored project lifecycle. Manages sponsor reporting deadlines, administrative milestones, and required deliverables to ensure timely submission of reports and documentation. Leads the preparation and review of interim, monthly, annual, and ad hoc research administration and financial reports for PIs and ARI Executive Leadership. Advises and supports PIs throughout the sponsored project lifecycle by providing guidance on sponsor requirements, research administration processes, and university procedures. Oversees and provides administrative guidance to personnel and PIs regarding proposal preparation, proposal routing, award setup, and grant administration activities throughout the sponsored project lifecycle. Manages sponsor communications related to administrative and compliance matters. Interprets sponsor financial requirements and advises PIs and project staff regarding allowable expenditures and required approvals. May supervise administrative and/or financial staff and oversee day-to-day operations as needed. Collaborates with the Project Manager III and other Finance personnel on matters that involve sponsored research administration, project management, and financial operations. Performs additional managerial, research administration, and special project duties as assigned. QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor's degree in Business Administration, Public Administration, Accounting, Finance, Research Administration, or a related field. Five (5) years of experience in sponsored research administration, grants management, research compliance, post-award administration, or a closely related field Additional education may be used as a substitution for the minimum experience requirement: Master’s degree in Public Administration, Accounting, Finance, Research Administration, or related field, and three (3) years of experience in sponsored research administration, grants management, research compliance, post-award administration, or a closely related field; OR Doctoral degree in Public Administration, Accounting, Finance, Research Administration, or related field, and one (1) year of experience in sponsored research administration, grants management, research compliance, post-award administration, or a closely related field SKILLS AND ABILITIES Experience leading teams and managing relationships with PIs, sponsors, project partners, and institutional stakeholders Experience coordinating sponsored research administration activities throughout the project lifecycle Strong interpersonal skills Strong written and verbal communication skills Intermediate proficiency in Microsoft Office Suite and database software (e.g., Smartsheet, Laserfiche, etc.) High level of articulation and professionalism Exceptional attention to detail Critical thinking and proactive problem solving Ability to take initiative and identify opportunities for improvement Ability to either work in a team environment or independently with minimal guidance Ability to manage and prioritize tasks, resources, and time effectively PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Master's degree in Business Administration Ten (10) years or more of experience in sponsored research administration, grants management, research compliance, post-award administration, or a closely related field Certified Research Administrator (CRA) certification or the ability to obtain certification Project Management Institute (PMI) Certification Knowledge of grants.gov and other federal, state, and foundation grant access points and submission portals Ability to train and advise faculty, staff, and students on project administration Experience working in higher education, higher education grants, and/or higher education research administration Experience administering federally funded research awards Familiarity with aviation, autonomous systems, unmanned aircraft systems (UAS), advanced air mobility (AAM), or related technology sectors. SALARY: $6,833.34 - $7,187.25 Monthly ($82,000.00 - $86,247.00 Annual, Approximately) NOTE: All ARI employees may utilize university vehicles for official business travel, including travel to the main campus, local meetings, events, training, and travel within Texas. Employees who operate university vehicles must possess and maintain a valid driver's licen se. Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. BENEFITS (rules, policies, eligibility apply) From our generous benefits package and professional development opportunities, to our retirement programs and our commitment to service excellence, the Island University is an engaging and rewarding place to work. INSURANCE: Medical $0 - $30 per month for Employee Only coverage after university contribution ($920 value). Up to 83% of premium covered by the university: Employee and Spouse Employee and Children Employee and Family coverage Dental & Vision Life Insurance, Accidental D&D, Long Term Disability, Flexible Spending Account and Day Care Spending Account DUAL CAREER PARTNER PROGRAM ON-CAMPUS WELLNESS OPPORTUNITIES Work Life Solutions: Counseling, Work Life Assistance, Financial Resources, and Legal Resources Tuition Benefits: Public Loan Forgiveness Book scholarships 100% tuition coverage for up to 18 credit hours per fiscal year (other rules may apply). Retirement : Teacher Retirement System of Texas (TRS) or Optional Retirement Plan (ORP) Voluntary Tax Deferred Account/Deferred Compensation Plan Time Off : 8+ hours of vacation paid time off every month. 8 hours of sick leave time off every month. 8 hours of paid time off for Birthday leave. 12-15 paid holidays each year. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Assistant-Director-of-Fitness-and-Wellbeing_R-096417-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Fitness and Wellbeing",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-24\nPosted: Posted 16 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Title Assistant Director of Fitness and Wellbeing Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department Recreational Sports Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Staff Job Description TAMU-CC is a dynamic university designated as both a Hispanic-Serving Institution (HSI) and Minority-Serving Institution (MSI) with approximately 11,000 students from 47 states and 54 foreign nations. We employ over 1,400 full-time and 2,000 part-time Islanders (including students/GAs). The University attracts highly talented faculty and staff and offers an array of undergraduate and graduate degrees, including doctoral programs.  As a member of the Texas A&M University System, TAMU-CC benefits from a range of resources, increased visibility and influence, and opportunities to collaborate in mutually beneficial ways with peers across member institutions and associated agencies.   TAMU-CC’s beautiful campus is located on a 240-acre island on Corpus Christi Bay and was ranked #1 College by the Sea by Best College Reviews. Our natural setting is enhanced by its modern, attractive, and state-of-the-art classroom buildings and support facilities. PURPOSE The position provides leadership and oversight for the collegiate recreation fitness program and empowers student staff to develop and foster fitness/wellness programs that promote student learning, development, and success . DESCRIPTION Functional Area 1: Fitness & Wellness Programming  Percent Effort: 40% Administer Fitness and Wellness Programs to include group exercise, personal training, wellness seminars, employee wellness program, beachfront small group trainings, student wellness program and special events for the University community at Texas A&M Corpus Christi.  Plans, coordinates, and leads presentations, classes, and workshops on health promotion/wellness topics and focuses on delivering programming that aligns with components of the dimensions of wellness. Collaborates with campus partners and compiles and produces a semester/annual calendar of wellness events during the academic year. Keep track of the client's progress and make necessary adjustments to the wellness plan to ensure continued success. Collaborates with various campus departments (i.e. nutrition, counseling center, etc.) to promote personal development and growth in the campus community. Develop, implement, and evaluate wellness programs to improve the overall health of employees or clients. Provide education and resources on nutrition, fitness, stress management, and other wellness topics. Some weekends and evenings as needed other duties as assigned. Functional Area 2: Planning/Administration Percent Effort: 30% Assist and lead departmental and university wide Wellness special events. Write, evaluate, implement, and support Fitness & Wellness policies for program. Gather participation numbers and demographic information for program enhancement. Serves in a leadership role on the University Wellness committee collaborating with campus partners and compiles and produces a semester/annual calendar of wellness events during the academic year for faculty/staff. Plans, coordinates, and leads presentations, classes, and workshops on health /wellness topics and focuses on delivering programming that aligns with components of the dimensions of wellness. Prepare, forecast, and monitor program-related budgets. Prepare and submit regular feedback, including quarterly and annual reports, financial reports, participation counts, goal updates and key performance indicators. Functional Area 3: Supervision Percent Effort: 30% Recruits, hires, trains, schedules, mentors, evaluates, and manages a graduate assistant, Weight-room trainer attendants, personal trainers, group Ex instructors and fitness staff in compliance with departmental and University regulations.  Progress clients through their corrective exercise programs in a safe and effective manner Serves as the subject matter expert on wellness topics, consulting with internal and external partners on the established strategic wellness initiatives. Assist in the training of the Fusion software package for the department and serve as the secondary point of contact for troubleshooting, training, and configuration.  Leads Campus Recreation’s student wellness initiatives. Creates, implements, and oversees wellness coaching program for students that focuses on creating healthy lifestyles. Assists in departmental risk management by coordinating, teaching, and overseeing certification courses in American Red Cross CPR/ AED/First Aid ensuring all personnel certifications are filed within the Wellness arena.   Oversees regular in-service trainings and meetings for Group Ex instructors’, Personal Trainers, Wellness coaches and Fitness attendants and ensures staff training is within compliance for operation.  QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor’s degree in Recreation or Sports Management Six (6) years of related experience. Additional education may be considered as a substitution for the minimum experience requirement. Master’s degree and four (4) years of related experience Experience related to fitness & wellness/event management and scheduling multiple spaces and programs. Experience with hiring and supervision of a support staff/team. Possess strong interpersonal, organizational and conflict management skills. Willingness to work weekends and evenings as needed. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Masters in Recreation / Student Affairs related areas. Group Fitness Instructor and Personal Trainer Certification by a nationally recognized agency Salary is commensurate upon education and/or experience. NOTE: Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. BENEFITS (rules, policies, eligibility apply) From our generous benefits package and professional development opportunities, to our retirement programs and our commitment to service excellence, the Island University is an engaging and rewarding place to work. Insurance Medical $0 - $30 per month for Employee Only coverage after university contribution ($920 value). Up to 83% of premium covered by the university: Employee and Spouse Employee and Children Employee and Family coverage Dental & Vision Life Insurance, Accidental D&D, Long Term Disability, Flexible Spending Account and Day Care Spending Account On-campus wellness opportunities Work Life Solutions: Counseling, Work Life Assistance, Financial Resources, and Legal Resources Tuition Benefits: Public Loan Forgiveness Book scholarships 100% tuition coverage for up to 18 credit hours per fiscal year (other rules may apply). Retirement Teacher Retirement System of Texas (TRS) or Optional Retirement Plan (ORP) Voluntary Tax Deferred Account/Deferred Compensation Plan Time Off: 8+ hours of vacation paid time off every month. 8 hours of sick leave time off every month. 8 hours of paid time off for Birthday leave. 12-15 paid holidays each year. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Operations-Coordinator_R-095789-1": {
      "title": "Operations Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-20\nPosted: Posted 20 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Title Operations Coordinator Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department Student Life Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Staff Job Description TAMU-CC is a dynamic university designated as both a Hispanic-Serving Institution (HSI) and Minority-Serving Institution (MSI) with approximately 11,000 students from 47 states and 54 foreign nations. We employ over 1,400 full-time and 2,000 part-time Islanders (including students/GAs). The University attracts highly talented faculty and staff and offers an array of undergraduate and graduate degrees, including doctoral programs.  As a member of the Texas A&M University System, TAMU-CC benefits from a range of resources, increased visibility and influence, and opportunities to collaborate in mutually beneficial ways with peers across member institutions and associated agencies.   TAMU-CC’s beautiful campus is located on a 240-acre island on Corpus Christi Bay and was ranked #1 College by the Sea by Best College Reviews. Our natural setting is enhanced by its modern, attractive, and state-of-the-art classroom buildings and support facilities. PURPOSE The Operations Coordinator serves as a key member of the University Center Operations team and is responsible for the daily operation of the Student Union, ensuring a safe, welcoming, and customer-focused environment for students, faculty, staff, and guests. The coordinator oversees facility operations, event setup and execution, student employee supervision, building inspections, risk management, technology support, and facility maintenance coordination. This position provides leadership during evenings and weekends, coordinates operational logistics for campus events, supervises student staff, and works collaboratively with campus partners to ensure exceptional customer service and successful event execution. The coordinator exercises independent judgment in managing daily facility operations and serves as the building manager during assigned shifts.   DESCRIPTION Functional Area 1: Facility Operations Percent Effort: 40%   Job Duties Coordinate the daily operation of the University Center. Conduct opening and closing building inspections. Ensure the facility is clean, safe, and operational. Monitor building systems and report maintenance concerns. Coordinate with Facilities Services regarding repairs and preventative maintenance. Assist with building access, keys, alarms, and security procedures. Maintain inventory of operational supplies and equipment. Inspect meeting rooms and public spaces for readiness, safety, and cleanliness. Perform routine facility inspections. Coordinate minor repairs. Monitor furniture condition. Report deferred maintenance issues. Assist with equipment replacement planning. Coordinate preventive maintenance schedules. Conduct routine safety inspections. Monitor emergency equipment. Ensure compliance with OSHA, ADA, fire safety, and university policies. Respond to facility emergencies. Complete incident and accident reports. Participate in emergency preparedness planning. Serve on emergency response teams when needed. Functional Area 2: Event Operations Percent Effort: 25%  Job Duties Coordinate operational logistics for meetings, conferences, and special events. Review event setup requirements and ensure accurate implementation. Supervise room setups and furniture configurations. Coordinate event changeovers. Ensure compliance with fire code, occupancy limits, and safety regulations. Troubleshoot operational issues during events. Provide after-hours support for evening and weekend programs. Assist with large-scale campus events, commencement activities, orientation, and signature university events. Assist with operation of meeting room technology. Troubleshoot audiovisual equipment. Support wireless presentation systems. Coordinate with Information Technology and AV staff regarding technology issues. Maintain operational technology inventory. Functional Area 3: Student Employment Percent Effort: 15%  Job Duties Recruit, hire, train, and supervise student operations staff. Develop work schedules and assign daily responsibilities. Conduct performance evaluations and coaching. Monitor payroll and timekeeping. Develop student leadership and customer service skills. Enforce departmental policies and safety procedures. Facilitate ongoing training related to operations, customer service, emergency response, and risk management. Functional Area 4: Breakers Game Room Percent Effort: 10% Job Duties Assist with daily operation of the game room. Coordinate equipment maintenance. Monitor inventory. Assist with tournaments and student programming.   Functional Area 5: Administrative Duties Percent Effort: 10% Job Duties Maintain operational records. Prepare reports and statistics. Assist with budgeting and purchasing. Monitor inventory and equipment replacement needs. Participate in departmental committees. Assist with policy and procedure development. Other duties as assigned. QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor’s degree Two (2) years in hospitality or related experience Proficiency in Microsoft Office Suite applications. Ability to work flexible hours, weekends and evenings on short notice. Experience with student union operations and services. Experience coordinating event logistics and support. Experience with facility management and building operations. Knowledge of customer service principles and best practices. Knowledge of risk management principles. Knowledge of building safety practices and procedures. Knowledge of fire and life safety regulations. Knowledge of Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) requirements. Knowledge of emergency response procedures. Experience operating basic audiovisual (AV) systems and equipment. Experience using event management and scheduling software. Ability to lead teams effectively. Ability to prioritize and manage multiple assignments simultaneously. Ability to communicate effectively and professionally, both verbally and in writing. Ability to analyze and resolve operational issues in a timely manner. Ability to establish and maintain positive working relationships with stakeholders. Ability to provide exceptional customer service in a fast-paced environment. Ability to exercise sound judgment and make effective decisions under pressure. Ability to manage competing priorities while meeting deadlines. Ability to work independently with minimal supervision. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Master’s degree, Experience supervising students NOTE:   Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. SALARY $3,833.34 Monthly $46,000 Annually Approximately BENEFITS (rules, policies, eligibility apply) From our generous benefits package and professional development opportunities, to our retirement programs and our commitment to service excellence, the Island University is an engaging and rewarding place to work. INSURANCE: Medical $0 - $30 per month for Employee Only coverage after university contribution ($920 value). Up to 83% of premium covered by the university: Employee and Spouse Employee and Children Employee and Family coverage Dental & Vision Life Insurance, Accidental D&D, Long Term Disability, Flexible Spending Account and Day Care Spending Account DUAL CAREER PARTNER PROGRAM ON-CAMPUS WELLNESS OPPORTUNITIES Work Life Solutions: Counseling, Work Life Assistance, Financial Resources, and Legal Resources Tuition Benefits: Public Loan Forgiveness Book scholarships 100% tuition coverage for up to 18 credit hours per fiscal year (other rules may apply). Retirement : Teacher Retirement System of Texas (TRS) or Optional Retirement Plan (ORP) Voluntary Tax Deferred Account/Deferred Compensation Plan Time Off : 8+ hours of vacation paid time off every month. 8 hours of sick leave time off every month. 8 hours of paid time off for Birthday leave. 12-15 paid holidays each year. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Director--Communications_R-096345-1": {
      "title": "Director, Communications",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-19\nPosted: Posted 21 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Title Director, Communications Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department Institutional Advancement Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Staff Job Description TAMU-CC is a dynamic university designated as both a Hispanic-Serving Institution (HSI) and Minority-Serving Institution (MSI) with approximately 11,000 students from 47 states and 54 foreign nations. We employ over 1,400 full-time and 2,000 part-time Islanders (including students/GAs). The University attracts highly talented faculty and staff and offers an array of undergraduate and graduate degrees, including doctoral programs.  As a member of the Texas A&M University System, TAMU-CC benefits from a range of resources, increased visibility and influence, and opportunities to collaborate in mutually beneficial ways with peers across member institutions and associated agencies.   TAMU-CC’s beautiful campus is located on a 240-acre island on Corpus Christi Bay and was ranked #1 College by the Sea by Best College Reviews. Our natural setting is enhanced by its modern, attractive, and state-of-the-art classroom buildings and support facilities. PURPOSE The Director of Communications provides strategic leadership for university communications, media relations, executive communications, crisis communications, and reputation management. Reporting to the Assistant Vice President of Marketing and Communications, the Director develops and executes communications strategies that advance institutional priorities and strengthen the university’s reputation among internal and external audiences. The Director serves as a senior communications advisor to university leadership, leads the university’s media relations and crisis communications efforts, and oversees the development of compelling institutional content and messaging. The position oversees a team of communication professionals and works collaboratively across the university to ensure communications are timely, coordinated, accurate, and aligned with institutional priorities, brand standards, and university values. RESPONSIBILITIES Functional Area 1: Strategic & Institutional Communications Percent Effort: 35% Develop and execute comprehensive communications strategies that advance university priorities, including enrollment, student success, research, advancement, community engagement, and institutional reputation. Establish and maintain a cohesive institutional voice and messaging framework across communications channels. Provide strategic communications counsel to university leadership on institutional initiatives, announcements, sensitive issues, and emerging opportunities. Develop executive communications, including speeches, talking points, presentations, statements, correspondence, and other leadership messaging. Oversee institutional communications and major publications, ensuring content is accurate, compelling, audience-focused, and aligned with university priorities. Collaborate with colleges, divisions, and university leadership to identify communications opportunities and coordinate messaging across the institution. Develop an overarching strategic communication plan and toolkit to support major university initiatives, announcements, events, and milestones. Provide communications guidance and standards to campus communicators to strengthen consistency and coordination across the university. Functional Area 2: Media Relations & Reputation Management Percent Effort: 25% Serve as the university’s primary media relations contact and spokesperson, as appropriate. Develop and maintain strong relationships with local, regional, national, and industry-specific media. Lead proactive media outreach and story development highlighting faculty expertise, research, student success, institutional accomplishments, community impact, and university priorities. Oversee press releases, media advisories, statements, feature stories, media pitches, and other external communications. Coordinate media interviews, press conferences, and media opportunities involving university leadership, faculty, staff, and subject-matter experts. Prepare university representatives for media interviews through messaging, talking points, briefing materials, and media preparation. Monitor media coverage, emerging issues, public sentiment, and reputational trends; provide analysis and recommendations to university leadership. Identify emerging reputational opportunities and risks and recommend proactive communications strategies. Functional Area 3: Crisis & Issues Communications Percent Effort: 20% Lead the university’s crisis and issues communications strategy in coordination with university leadership and appropriate campus partners. Develop, maintain, and regularly evaluate the university’s crisis communications plan, protocols, templates, and response procedures. Serve as the communications lead within the university’s Incident Command structure during emergencies and significant incidents. Develop and coordinate timely, accurate communications for internal and external audiences across email, web, social media, media relations, emergency notification systems, and other channels. Advise university leadership on communications strategy during emergencies, sensitive situations, and reputational issues. Coordinate messaging with university police, university leadership, legal counsel, the Texas A&M University System, and other appropriate partners. Monitor media, social media, and public conversation during emerging issues and crises and recommend appropriate responses. Conduct post-incident communications reviews and incorporate lessons learned into future crisis planning. Maintain appropriate after-hours availability for time-sensitive emergencies and reputational matters. Functional Area 4: Content Strategy & Editorial Leadership Percent Effort: 10% Provide strategic direction for university news, institutional storytelling, executive communications, and other communications content. Oversee the development of written content for digital, social, video, audio, print, and other communications channels. Establish editorial priorities that support institutional goals and strengthen the university’s reputation. Serve as final editorial authority for designated university-level communications, ensuring accuracy, consistency, AP style compliance, and alignment with institutional messaging. Partner with Marketing, Creative Services, Web, Enrollment, Advancement, academic units, and other university teams to coordinate integrated storytelling and communications. Identify opportunities to elevate faculty expertise and university thought leadership in regional, national, and industry conversations. Functional Area 5: Communications Measurement & Strategy Percent Effort: 5% Establish annual communications goals, key performance indicators, and measures of success aligned with the university’s strategic priorities. Monitor and evaluate media coverage, audience engagement, digital performance, sentiment, and other relevant communications metrics. Use data and audience insights to evaluate communications effectiveness and refine strategies. Provide regular communications and reputation reports, insights, and recommendations to university leadership. Functional Area 6: Leadership & Administration Percent Effort: 5% Lead, supervise, mentor, and develop professional communications staff. Establish team priorities, workflows, performance expectations, and professional development goals. Foster a collaborative, responsive, high-performing culture focused on quality, service, innovation, and continuous improvement. Manage assigned communications budgets and resources. Develop and maintain communications policies, procedures, editorial standards, and standard operating procedures. Serve on university committees, task forces, emergency response teams, and working groups as assigned. Represent the university at professional, community, and industry events as appropriate. Perform other duties as assigned. QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor’s degree in communications, public relations, journalism, marketing, or a closely related field. Ten (10) years of progressively responsible professional experience in communications, public relations, media relations, journalism, or a related field, including at least three (3) years of supervisory or leadership experience. Additional education/experience may be considered as a substitute for the minimum requirements: Master’s degree in communications, public relations, journalism, marketing, or a closely related field with eight (8) years of progressively responsible professional experience in communications, public relations, media relations, journalism, or a related field, including at least three (3) years of supervisory or leadership experience Doctoral degree in communications, public relations, journalism, marketing, or a closely related field with six (6) years of progressively responsible professional experience in communications, public relations, media relations, journalism, or a related field, including at least three (3) years of supervisory or leadership experience Exceptional written, verbal, editing, and interpersonal communication skills. Demonstrated experience developing and implementing strategic communications plans for complex organizations. Demonstrated experience working with media and developing successful media relations strategies. Experience managing sensitive, high-profile, or crisis communications. Strong judgment and demonstrated ability to advise leaders on complex communications matters. Ability to manage multiple priorities and make sound decisions in a fast-paced environment. Demonstrated ability to use communications data and analytics to inform strategy and evaluate effectiveness. Ability to maintain the security and integrity of critical infrastructure, which may include communications systems, computer networks and systems, cybersecurity systems, electrical grid, hazardous waste treatment system or water treatment system. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Master’s degree in communications, public relations, journalism, marketing, higher education administration, or a related field. Communications or public relations experience within higher education or another large, complex organization. Experience leading crisis and issues communications. Experience providing communications counsel to executive leadership. Experience developing regional or national media relationships and successfully pitching stories. Knowledge of emerging communications trends, media monitoring, digital communications, and reputation management. SALARY: $85,000 - $100,000/annually, approximately $7,083.34 - $8,333.34/monthly NOTE: Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. BENEFITS (rules, policies, eligibility apply) From our generous benefits package and professional development opportunities, to our retirement programs and our commitment to service excellence, the Island University is an engaging and rewarding place to work. Insurance Medical $0 - $30 per month for Employee Only coverage after university contribution ($920 value). Up to 83% of premium covered by the university: Employee and Spouse Employee and Children Employee and Family coverage Dental & Vision Life Insurance, Accidental D&D, Long Term Disability, Flexible Spending Account and Day Care Spending Account On-campus wellness opportunities Work Life Solutions: Counseling, Work Life Assistance, Financial Resources, and Legal Resources Tuition Benefits: Public Loan Forgiveness Book scholarships 100% tuition coverage for up to 18 credit hours per fiscal year (other rules may apply). Retirement Teacher Retirement System of Texas (TRS) or Optional Retirement Plan (ORP) Voluntary Tax Deferred Account/Deferred Compensation Plan Time Off: 8+ hours of vacation paid time off every month. 8 hours of sick leave time off every month. 8 hours of paid time off for Birthday leave. 12-15 paid holidays each year. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
