    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Assistant-Clinical-Professor--Nursing-Practice_R-096919-2": {
      "title": "Assistant Clinical Professor, Nursing Practice",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Title Assistant Clinical Professor, Nursing Practice Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department College Of Nursing and Health Sciences Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Faculty Job Description The College of Nursing and Health Sciences (CONHS) at Texas A&M University-Corpus Christi (TAMU-CC) invites applications for a non-tenure track Assistant Clinical Professor of Nursing Practice to begin as early as Spring 2027. This is a 9-month academic appointment with a possible summer appointment and will be located at the Corpus Christi campus. Faculty members are required to be physically on campus as needed to contribute to academic mission of the College and University. Job Description The CONHS non-tenure-track faculty responsibilities include teaching, service and implementation and evaluation of nursing courses and student learning. Faculty are expected to model exemplar professional behavior through work with students, peers, and community colleagues and to provide student support to include mentoring, advising, recruiting, FNP student clinical placement, and DNP student project supervision. Faculty are also expected to participate in activities that contribute to achieving TAMU-CC and CONHS organizational goals. Teaching responsibilities include working in collaboration with department chair, program director and program faculty to ensure quality program curriculum and instruction in the didactic and clinical phases of the program. Duties include course management, teaching, skills and simulation labs, student advisement, and student formative and summative evaluation. Responsibilities may also include participation in clinical site visits, scholarly project committee work, student remediation, test writing and analysis, program admissions, accreditation processes and program assessment to include serving on in-person faculty meetings and committees essential to the Family Nurse Practitioner and Doctor of Nursing Practice programs, college, and university’s functioning. Texas A&M University-Corpus Christi is a vibrant, Hispanic and Minority Serving Doctoral Research Institution that proudly provides a solid academic reputation, renowned faculty, and highly rated degree programs since 1947. The University has a heritage of teaching excellence with innovation in research and community engagement as part of the distinguished Texas A&M System. With palm tree-lined pathways throughout the campus, nearby natural wetlands, a scenic hike-and-bike trail and a university beach, Texas A&M University-Corpus Christi is the only university in the nation located on its own island, at the heart of the Texas Gulf Coast. Required Qualifications: Master’s in Nursing from an accredited institution. Minimum of two (2) years of clinical nursing experience. Current unencumbered RN License and/or APRN License in the State of Texas or eligible for licensure in Texas at time of hire. Current board certification as an FNP from AANP or ANCC Preferred Qualifications: One to two (1 -2) years of academic teaching experience Earned doctorate in nursing (PhD, DNP) or related field (e.g., EdD) Experience with classroom/didactic course teaching. Knowledge of curriculum and curriculum development. Knowledge of National Organization of Nurse Practitioner Faculties (NONPF) Competencies for nurse practitioners (NPs). Experience in program coordination and management, including curriculum refinement and student progress. Experience with web-based course delivery, including on-line course development, online teaching, and application of best practices in online education. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively across disciplines and with community practice partners. TO APPLY: http://hr.tamucc.edu/careers/ All required documentation must be submitted to be considered for the position. A completed application will include: A letter of application addressing qualifications listed and a teaching philosophy statement. A current curriculum vitae. Three (3) professional references including name, address, title, telephone number, and email addresses- including a previous or current supervisor.  Copies of unofficial transcripts for undergraduate and graduate coursework. The job posting will remain open until a finalist is selected. Applications will be reviewed immediately. NOTE: Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Assistant-Professor--Healthcare-Administration_R-096910-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Healthcare Administration",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Title Assistant Professor, Healthcare Administration Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department College Of Nursing and Health Sciences Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Faculty Job Description The College of Nursing and Health Sciences (CONHS) at Texas A&M University-Corpus Christi (TAMU-CC) invites applications for a tenure track Assistant Professor of Health Sciences in Healthcare Administration (HCAD) to begin as early as Fall 2027. This is a 9-month academic appointment with a possible summer appointment and will be located at the Corpus Christi campus. Faculty members are required to be physically on campus as needed to contribute to academic mission of the College and University. Job Description We are seeking candidates with expertise in healthcare administration who demonstrate teaching and/or research expertise across multiple areas relevant to the program, including healthcare information systems, artificial intelligence and emerging technologies in healthcare, project management, health law, and health services research. Tenure-track faculty are expected to teach students, pursue a robust program of research, seek external funding, and disseminate research findings. Faculty are expected to model exemplary professional behavior through work with students, peers, administration, and community colleagues and to provide student support including mentoring, advising, recruiting, and HCAD student project supervision. Teaching responsibilities ensures adherence to program policies, course objectives, program outcomes and current teaching, experiential, and applied learning best practices. All faculty are expected to maintain content expertise, and to participate in college, university and professional service activities. Active participation in College and University committees and activities is required. The successful candidate will teach undergraduate Healthcare Administration courses and, as appropriate, contribute to graduate-level teaching within the College. The faculty member will develop and maintain an active program of scholarship, with scholarly work disseminated through peer-reviewed publications, presentations, and other appropriate venues. The successful candidate will foster relationships with healthcare organizations and community partners, support the development of internships and other experiential learning opportunities, contribute to program assessment and continuous improvement, and participate in activities that advance the goals of CONHS and TAMU-CC. Texas A&M University-Corpus Christi is a vibrant, Hispanic Serving Doctoral Research Institution with a strong academic reputation, accomplished faculty, and highly regarded degree programs. Since 1947, the University has built a tradition of excellence in teaching, research, innovation, and community engagement as a member of The Texas A&M University System. Located on its own island in the heart of the Texas Gulf Coast, TAMU-CC offers a distinctive learning and working environment surrounded by natural wetlands, scenic trails, beaches, and bays. Required Qualifications A PhD in Healthcare Administration, Health Sciences, or a closely related field relevant to healthcare administration. While advanced ABD will be considered, the degree must be conferred at the time of appointment. Evidence of scholarly activity commensurate with the tenure track expectations of the College and University. Demonstrated capacity to teach undergraduate courses in Healthcare Administration across multiple content areas. Preferred Qualifications At least one (1) year of teaching experience at the baccalaureate and/or graduate level. Demonstrated ability to collaborate effectively with colleagues across disciplines and with healthcare organizations and community partners. Evidence of an active or developing program of research, including peer- reviewed scholarly publications and potential for external funding. Experience developing and teaching high -quality online courses. Teaching, research, and/or professional experience across one or more areas of healthcare administration, including healthcare information systems, artificial intelligence and emerging technologies in healthcare, project management, health law, and health services research. Experience supporting student experiential learning through internships, applied projects, or partnerships with healthcare organizations. All required documentation must be submitted to be considered for the position. TO APPLY: http://hr.tamucc.edu/careers A completed application will include: A letter of application addressing the qualifications listed, including a statement of teaching/research philosophy. A curriculum vitae. Three professional references including name, address, title, telephone number, and email addresses. Copies of unofficial transcripts for undergraduate and graduate coursework. The job posting will remain open until a finalist has been selected. Applicants are encouraged to apply by December 1, 2026 . NOTE:  Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Assistant-Clinical-Professor-of-Nursing-Education_R-096780-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Clinical Professor of Nursing Education",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Title Assistant Clinical Professor of Nursing Education Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department College Of Nursing Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Faculty Job Description The College of Nursing and Health Sciences (CONHS) at Texas A&M University-Corpus Christi (TAMU-CC) invites applications for a non-tenure track Assistant Clinical Professor of Nursing Education to begin as early as Spring 2027. This is a 9-month academic appointment with a possible summer appointment and will be located at the Corpus Christi campus. Faculty members are required to be physically on campus as needed to contribute to academic mission of the College and University. Job Description The CONHS non-tenure-track faculty responsibilities include teaching, service and implementation and evaluation of nursing courses and student learning. Faculty are expected to model exemplar professional behavior through work with students, peers, and community colleagues and to provide student support to include mentoring, advising, recruiting, and BSN prelicensure student clinical placement. Faculty are also expected to participate in activities that contribute to achieving TAMUCC and CONHS organizational goals. Teaching responsibilities include working in collaboration with academic program coordinators, and program faculty to ensure quality program curriculum and instruction in the didactic and clinical phases of the program. Duties include course management, teaching, skills and simulation labs, student advisement, and student formative and summative evaluation. Responsibilities may also include participation in clinical site visits, committee work, student remediation, test writing and analysis, program admissions, accreditation processes and program assessment to include serving on in-person faculty meetings and committees essential to the prelicensure undergraduate nursing programs (BSN), college, and university’s functioning. Texas A&M University-Corpus Christi is a vibrant, Hispanic and Minority Serving R2 Doctoral Research Institution that proudly provides a solid academic reputation, renowned faculty, and highly rated degree programs since 1947. The University has a heritage of teaching excellence with innovation in research and community engagement as part of the distinguished Texas A&M System. TAMU-CC is the only university in the nation located on its own island, at the heart of the Texas Gulf Coast. With palm tree lined pathways throughout the campus, nearby natural wetlands, a scenic hike-and-bike trail and pristine views of the beach and bays, Texas A&M University-Corpus Christi is a first-choice institution. Required Qualifications: Master’s in Nursing from an accredited institution. Minimum of two (2) years of clinical nursing experience. Current unencumbered RN License and/or APRN License in the State of Texas or eligible for licensure in Texas at time of hire. If APRN, current board certification is an FNP is required from AANP or ANCC Preferred Qualifications: One to two (1 -2) years of academic teaching experience Experience with classroom/didactic course teaching or participating in health care simulations. Nursing practice specialty certification (e.g., CNE, RNC-OB, CHSE) Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively across disciplines and with community practice partners. TO APPLY: http://hr.tamucc.edu/careers/ All required documentation must be submitted to be considered for the position. A completed application will include: A letter of application addressing qualifications listed and a teaching philosophy statement. A current curriculum vitae. Three (3) professional references including name, address, title, telephone number, and email addresses- including a previous or current supervisor. Copies of unofficial transcripts for undergraduate and graduate coursework. The job posting will remain open until a finalist is selected. Applications will be reviewed immediately. NOTE: Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Simulation---Clinical-Learning-Center-Lab-Coordinator_R-096923": {
      "title": "Simulation & Clinical Learning Center Lab Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Title Simulation & Clinical Learning Center Lab Coordinator Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department College Of Nursing and Health Sciences Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Staff Job Description TAMU-CC is a dynamic university designated as both a Hispanic-Serving Institution (HSI) and Minority-Serving Institution (MSI) with approximately 11,000 students from 47 states and 54 foreign nations. We employ over 1,400 full-time and 2,000 part-time Islanders (including students/GAs). The University attracts highly talented faculty and staff and offers an array of undergraduate and graduate degrees, including doctoral programs.  As a member of the Texas A&M University System, TAMU-CC benefits from a range of resources, increased visibility and influence, and opportunities to collaborate in mutually beneficial ways with peers across member institutions and associated agencies.   TAMU-CC’s beautiful campus is located on a 240-acre island on Corpus Christi Bay and was ranked #1 College by the Sea by Best College Reviews. Our natural setting is enhanced by its modern, attractive, and state-of-the-art classroom buildings and support facilities. PURPOSE The Simulation & Clinical Learning Center (SCLC) Lab Coordinator supports the day-to-day technical, logistical, and operational functions of the SCLC. RESPONSIBILITIES Operations - 80% Simulation Environment Setup and Technical Operations Perform the setup and breakdown of all simulation environments and skills labs, including preparation of task trainers, props, supplies, and all associated equipment to ensure environments are ready for scheduled activities. Maintain simulation spaces in a clean, organized, and clinically realistic condition at all times, ensuring that environments meet the standards required for effective experiential learning. Report equipment concerns, damage, or operational issues to the SCLC Lab Manager or simulation technician promptly to ensure timely resolution and continued readiness of simulation resources. Open Lab Operations Facilitate Open Lab sessions by preparing the environment, ensuring equipment availability, and maintaining the operational integrity of the space to support students in meeting their learning objectives outside of scheduled coursework. Coordinate with the SCLC Lab Manager and SCLC staff to resolve scheduling conflicts, manage room turnovers, and ensure that all simulation spaces are properly reset between sessions. Inventory, Supply, and Resource Management Assist with the management, stocking, and maintenance of all SCLC inventory, including simulation supplies, consumables, equipment, and task trainers, in compliance with SCLC operating policies. Conduct regular inventory counts and maintain accurate records of supply levels, equipment status, and resource utilization, reporting findings to the SCLC Lab Manager. Ensure that all simulation materials are stored, labeled, and managed in compliance with SIM OP 1.010 (Separation of Simulation and Actual Patient Care Materials) and applicable SCLC operating policies. Certification Programs and Safety Compliance Support the SCLC's American Heart Association (AHA) Basic Life Support (BLS) program, including maintaining BLS certification records, and ensuring training materials and equipment are available and ready for use. Adhere to all applicable safety policies and procedures governing the use, handling, and storage of simulation equipment, supplies, and materials within the SCLC. Coordination - 15% Scheduling Schedule skills labs, simulation sessions, and Open Lab sessions using scheduling software as directed by the SCLC Lab Manager, ensuring equitable use of spaces and resources across all courses and programs according to SCLC policy. Supply Orders and Procurement Support Process supply and equipment orders as directed by the SCLC Lab Manager, following TAMU-CC procurement procedures and ensuring timely restocking of consumable materials. Data Collection and Accreditation Support Collect and maintain data representing SCLC operational activities, including room utilization, equipment usage, supply consumption, and certification program records, as directed by the SCLC Lab Manager. Contribute to accreditation documentation and reporting activities in support of SSH Accreditation and other institutional quality assurance requirements, as directed by the SCLC Lab Manager. Other - 5% Provide tours of the SCLC to prospective students, families, educators, donors, and community members, representing the center professionally and accurately. Perform other duties as assigned by the SCLC Lab Manager. QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor’s degree in Nursing from a regionally accredited institution Minimum two (2) years of experience in clinical nursing practice in an acute care setting. Additional experience may be considered as a substitute for the minimum education requirement: Associate’s degree in Nursing with four (4) years of experience in clinical nursing practice in an acute care setting. Current unrestricted Texas Registered Nurse (RN) license is required. American Heart Association (AHA) certification and Basic Life Support (BLS) certification required. Experience operating, troubleshooting, and maintaining laboratory, clinical, or simulation equipment. Strong organizational, time management, verbal, and written communication skills. Ability to manage multiple priorities while working collaboratively with faculty, staff, students, and community partners Ability to maintain the security and integrity of critical infrastructure, which may include communications systems, computer networks and systems, cybersecurity systems, electrical grid, hazardous waste treatment system or water treatment system. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience in a healthcare simulation environment. CHSOS or CHSE certification, or willingness to obtain certification when eligible. Experience with medical/simulation equipment. Experience with inventory management, supply chain coordination, or procurement. SALARY: up to $53,560.08/annually, approximately $4,463.34/monthly NOTE: Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. BENEFITS (rules, policies, eligibility apply) From our generous benefits package and professional development opportunities, to our retirement programs and our commitment to service excellence, the Island University is an engaging and rewarding place to work. Insurance Medical $0 - $30 per month for Employee Only coverage after university contribution ($920 value). Up to 83% of premium covered by the university: Employee and Spouse Employee and Children Employee and Family coverage Dental & Vision Life Insurance, Accidental D&D, Long Term Disability, Flexible Spending Account and Day Care Spending Account On-campus wellness opportunities Work Life Solutions: Counseling, Work Life Assistance, Financial Resources, and Legal Resources Tuition Benefits: Public Loan Forgiveness Book scholarships 100% tuition coverage for up to 18 credit hours per fiscal year (other rules may apply). Retirement Teacher Retirement System of Texas (TRS) or Optional Retirement Plan (ORP) Voluntary Tax Deferred Account/Deferred Compensation Plan Time Off: 8+ hours of vacation paid time off every month. 8 hours of sick leave time off every month. 8 hours of paid time off for Birthday leave. 12-15 paid holidays each year. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Director-of-Marketing_R-096903-1": {
      "title": "Director of Marketing",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Title Director of Marketing Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department Institutional Advancement Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Staff Job Description TAMU-CC is a dynamic university designated as both a Hispanic-Serving Institution (HSI) and Minority-Serving Institution (MSI) with approximately 11,000 students from 47 states and 54 foreign nations. We employ over 1,400 full-time and 2,000 part-time Islanders (including students/GAs). The University attracts highly talented faculty and staff and offers an array of undergraduate and graduate degrees, including doctoral programs.  As a member of the Texas A&M University System, TAMU-CC benefits from a range of resources, increased visibility and influence, and opportunities to collaborate in mutually beneficial ways with peers across member institutions and associated agencies.   TAMU-CC’s beautiful campus is located on a 240-acre island on Corpus Christi Bay and was ranked #1 College by the Sea by Best College Reviews. Our natural setting is enhanced by its modern, attractive, and state-of-the-art classroom buildings and support facilities. PURPOSE The Director of Marketing provides strategic leadership for the university’s integrated marketing efforts, with primary responsibility for institutional marketing strategy, enrollment marketing and communications, advertising and paid media, Auxiliary Services marketing, and trademarks and licensing. Reporting to the Assistant Vice President of Marketing and Communications, the Director develops and implements data-informed strategies to advance institutional priorities, strengthen the university’s market position, support enrollment and revenue goals, and maximize the effectiveness of the university's marketing investments. The Director leads a team of marketing professionals and works collaboratively with Creative Services, Communications, Web Services, Enrollment Management, Auxiliary Services, academic units, and other campus partners to ensure marketing efforts are strategically prioritized, audience-focused, measurable, and aligned with the university’s overall brand and institutional goals. DESCRIPTION Functional Area 1: Strategic Marketing & Brand Leadership Percent Effort: 25% Develop and implement integrated marketing strategies that advance institutional priorities, including student recruitment, enrollment, reputation, community engagement, and revenue-generating initiatives. Translate institutional goals and priorities into audience-focused marketing strategies, campaigns, and measurable objectives. Provide strategic marketing counsel and recommendations to university leadership and campus partners. Identify market opportunities, audience trends, competitive positioning, and emerging marketing strategies that can strengthen university performance and visibility. Establish marketing priorities and recommend allocation of resources based on institutional goals, audience needs, anticipated impact, and available resources. Partner with Creative Services, Communications, and Web Services to translate marketing strategy into coordinated campaigns across paid, owned, and earned channels. Ensure university marketing strategies and campaigns consistently support and strengthen the university’s overall brand positioning. Promote an integrated, proactive approach to university marketing that prioritizes strategic institutional needs and measurable outcomes. Functional Area 2:  Enrollment Marketing & Communications Percent Effort: 25% Lead the development and implementation of integrated enrollment marketing and communications strategies in partnership with Enrollment Management. Develop audience-specific strategies supporting prospective students throughout the recruitment, application, admission, and enrollment journey. Provide strategic oversight for recruitment marketing campaigns, enrollment communications, CRM strategy, digital engagement, and related marketing initiatives. Partner with Enrollment Management to establish target audiences, segmentation strategies, recruitment priorities, communication plans, and performance goals. Use enrollment data, CRM engagement, digital behavior, market research, and campaign performance to continuously refine recruitment strategies. Identify opportunities to improve the effectiveness, personalization, timing, and coordination of prospective student communications. Coordinate with Creative Services, Communications, and Web Services to ensure enrollment campaigns deliver consistent and compelling experiences across channels. Establish measures of success and regularly evaluate the effectiveness of enrollment marketing and communications efforts. Functional Area 3: Advertising, Media & Marketing Performance    Percent Effort: 20% Provide strategic oversight of university advertising and paid media across digital, traditional, social, sponsorship, and emerging channels. Develop annual media strategies and investment priorities based on institutional objectives, target audiences, market opportunities, and available resources. Oversee media planning, placement, targeting, optimization, and vendor relationships for university-level advertising. Establish goals, key performance indicators, and measurement frameworks for advertising campaigns and other major university marketing initiatives. Evaluate campaign performance, conversion, audience engagement, market awareness, web traffic, and other relevant measures of effectiveness. Use market research, audience insights, campaign analytics, competitive analysis, and industry trends to optimize advertising and marketing strategies. Develop regular performance reporting that communicates results, insights, trends, and recommendations to university leadership and campus partners. Monitor university advertising budgets, media buys, vendor contracts, and related expenditures to maximize effectiveness and return on investment. Identify emerging marketing technologies, platforms, audience behaviors, and advertising opportunities that can improve university marketing performance. Functional Area 4: Auxiliary Services, Licensing & Revenue-Generating Marketing Percent Effort: 15% Provide strategic marketing leadership and oversight for Auxiliary Services, including Housing, and other designated revenue-generating university operations. Partner with Auxiliary Services leadership to establish marketing priorities supporting occupancy, participation, utilization, revenue, and other business objectives. Ensure Auxiliary Services marketing efforts align with the broader university brand and institutional marketing strategy. Provide strategic oversight of the university’s trademark and licensing program to protect and strengthen the university brand while identifying opportunities for growth. Ensure university trademarks and licensed products are managed in accordance with institutional and system policies and established brand standards. Develop strategies to expand licensing opportunities, strengthen vendor and retailer relationships, increase university visibility, and support licensing revenue growth. Evaluate marketing and licensing performance and identify opportunities to strengthen revenue-generating initiatives through audience insights, partnerships, and strategic promotion. Functional Area 5: Team Leadership, Partnerships & Resource Management Percent Effort: 15% Lead, supervise, mentor, and develop professional marketing staff. Establish team priorities, workflows, performance expectations, and professional development goals aligned with institutional and departmental priorities. Foster a collaborative, strategic, and high-performing culture focused on innovation, accountability, service, and measurable results. Manage assigned marketing, advertising, licensing, and program budgets and resources. Oversee vendor relationships, contracts, and external marketing partnerships within assigned areas. Build effective partnerships across academic and administrative units and serve as a strategic marketing resource to the university community. Collaborate closely with Creative Services, Communications, and Web Services leadership to establish priorities, coordinate resources, and ensure effective execution of university marketing initiatives. Serve on university committees, task forces, and working groups as assigned. Represent the university at professional, community, and industry events as appropriate. Perform other duties as assigned. QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor’s degree in marketing, communications, business, advertising, or a closely related field. Ten (10) years of progressively responsible professional experience in marketing, advertising, enrollment marketing, communications, or a related field, including demonstrated leadership or supervisory experience. Additional education may be considered as a substitute for the minimum experience requirement: Master’s degree in marketing, communications, business, advertising, or a closely related field with eight (8) years of progressively responsible professional experience in marketing, advertising, enrollment marketing, communications, or a related field, including demonstrated leadership or supervisory experience Doctoral degree in marketing, communications, business, advertising, or a closely related field with six (6) years of progressively responsible professional experience in marketing, advertising, enrollment marketing, communications, or a related field, including demonstrated leadership or supervisory experience Demonstrated experience developing and executing integrated marketing strategies and large-scale campaigns. Experience developing enrollment, recruitment, audience, or lifecycle marketing strategies. Experience managing paid media strategies, advertising budgets, and vendor relationships. Demonstrated ability to use data, analytics, market research, and performance measures to inform marketing strategy and resource allocation. Strong leadership, collaboration, communication, and strategic planning skills. Demonstrated ability to manage multiple priorities, budgets, teams, and complex initiatives in a fast-paced organization. Strong understanding of digital marketing, audience segmentation, campaign optimization, CRM-based communications, and emerging marketing trends. Demonstrated ability to build collaborative relationships and translate organizational priorities into effective marketing strategies. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Master’s degree in marketing, communications, business, higher education administration, or a related field. Marketing experience within higher education or another large, complex organization. Experience developing enrollment or student recruitment marketing strategies. Experience with CRM-based communications and student lifecycle marketing. Experience overseeing integrated advertising and media strategies across multiple channels. Experience with trademark licensing or collegiate licensing programs. Experience supporting marketing for revenue-generating programs, services, or business operations. SALARY: $85,000 - $95,000/annually, approximately $7,083.34 - $7,916.67/monthly NOTE: Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. BENEFITS (rules, policies, eligibility apply) From our generous benefits package and professional development opportunities, to our retirement programs and our commitment to service excellence, the Island University is an engaging and rewarding place to work. Insurance Medical $0 - $30 per month for Employee Only coverage after university contribution ($920 value). Up to 83% of premium covered by the university: Employee and Spouse Employee and Children Employee and Family coverage Dental & Vision Life Insurance, Accidental D&D, Long Term Disability, Flexible Spending Account and Day Care Spending Account On-campus wellness opportunities Work Life Solutions: Counseling, Work Life Assistance, Financial Resources, and Legal Resources Tuition Benefits: Public Loan Forgiveness Book scholarships 100% tuition coverage for up to 18 credit hours per fiscal year (other rules may apply). Retirement Teacher Retirement System of Texas (TRS) or Optional Retirement Plan (ORP) Voluntary Tax Deferred Account/Deferred Compensation Plan Time Off: 8+ hours of vacation paid time off every month. 8 hours of sick leave time off every month. 8 hours of paid time off for Birthday leave. 12-15 paid holidays each year. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Assistant-Associate-Professor-of-English--Writing-Studies_R-097131-2": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of English, Writing Studies",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nJob Title Assistant/Associate Professor of English, Writing Studies Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department College Of Liberal Arts Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Faculty Job Description The Department of English at Texas A&M University–Corpus Christi invites applications for a 9-month, tenure-track faculty position in Rhetoric and Composition, beginning Fall 2027. The appointment may be made at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor, commensurate with the candidate’s qualifications, level of expertise, and professional experience. The department is seeking an experienced writing program administrator to support the First-Year Writing Program. Applicants should demonstrate a record of excellent teaching and have an active, current scholarly agenda in writing program administration. Teaching expectations will include a 3/3 course load (not including course release time for program administration) comprising first-year writing courses and upper-division undergraduate courses in writing studies. Candidates will also have the opportunity to offer graduate courses and mentor students in the department’s English MA program.  Administrative duties, when part of the faculty’s appointment, will include development and assessment of writing curricula and outcomes and initiating faculty development opportunities to support writing pedagogy in the program. The Department of English offers undergraduate English majors a choice in either a Writing or Literary Studies emphasis, curricula for secondary English certification, certificates in Writing for Non-Profits, social media, and TESOL, and minors in Technical and Professional Writing, Creative Writing, and Literary Studies. The graduate program provides an interdisciplinary educational experience in a generalist MA in English by requiring advanced study in Writing Studies, Literary Studies, and Applied Linguistics. At TAMU-CC, our first-year writing program is informed by research on how people write, how they learn, and what they need to be flexible, adept writers ready to take on new challenges. Our program works to teach writing in ways that connect students to their majors and coordinate with the demands of other first-year classes. As a program, we want to help students understand that writing, language, and rhetoric are all multifaceted and evolving; we want students to be able to use writing and language in ways that will help them achieve their goals. Texas A&M University-Corpus Christi is a vibrant, Hispanic and Minority Serving R2 Doctoral Research Institution that proudly provides a solid academic reputation, renowned faculty, and highly rated degree programs since 1947. The University has a heritage of teaching excellence with innovation in research and community engagement as part of the distinguished Texas A&M System. The only university in the nation is located on its own island, at the heart of the Texas Gulf Coast. With palm tree lined pathways throughout the campus, nearby natural wetlands, a scenic hike-and-bike trail and pristine views of the beach and bays, Texas A&M University-Corpus Christi is a first-choice institution. Required Qualifications: Assistant Rank: Doctoral degree in Rhetoric and Composition, Writing Studies or related discipline .     Record of active scholarly production or research in, or related to, writing program administration. Record of quality teaching in one or more of the following: Writing Studies, First-Year Writing, and/or Technical and Professional Writing. Demonstrated experience in writing program administration. Associate Rank: Doctoral degree in Rhetoric and Composition, Writing Studies or related discipline.  Record of active scholarly production or research in, or related to, writing program administration. Record of quality teaching in one or more of the following: Writing Studies, First-Year Writing, and/or Technical and Professional Writing.   Five (5) years of full-time university faculty experience including at least four (4) years in the rank of assistant professor. Related professional experience may substitute in rare cases. Demonstrated experience in writing program administration. Preferred Qualifications: Demonstrated evidence of culturally responsive pedagogy, especially in the context of a regional university designated as a Hispanic-Serving Institution Demonstrated evidence of an ability to teach in face-to-face and online modalities Experience teaching and advising first-generation students and historically underserved communities. Experience in establishing and assessing writing program outcomes TO APPLY : https://www.tamucc.edu/human-resources/careers/index.php All required documentation must be submitted to be considered for the position. A completed application will include: A letter of interest addressing qualifications listed, including research agenda and teaching philosophy; A statement of writing program administration philosophy; A current curriculum vitae. Unofficial copies of transcripts of graduate coursework. Three professional references including name, address, title, telephone number, and email addresses. Candidates whose applications are selected for further review may be asked to provide additional application materials, including a research statement, sample syllabus for a first-year writing course, and writing samples. Application deadline: Thursday, October 15th, 2026 Position start date: Fall 2027 For questions about the position, please contact Dr. Christopher Andrews, Department Chair, at christopher.andrews@tamucc.edu . NOTE: Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Assistant-Professor-of-English--Applied-Linguistics_R-096912-2": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of English, Applied Linguistics",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nJob Title Assistant Professor of English, Applied Linguistics Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department College Of Liberal Arts Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Faculty Job Description The Department of English at Texas A&M University–Corpus Christi invites applications for a 9-month, tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor of English, with a concentration in Applied Linguistics, beginning in Fall 2027. The position entails a 3/3 teaching load and has commensurate research and service expectations. Summer and online teaching opportunities may be available. The Department of English allows English majors to pursue undergraduate coursework with either a Writing or Literary Studies emphasis. The department additionally offers curricula for secondary English certification; certificates in Writing for Non-Profits, social media, and TESOL; and minors in Technical and Professional Writing, Creative Writing, and Literary Studies. The graduate program provides an interdisciplinary educational experience by requiring coursework in Literary Studies, Writing Studies, and Applied Linguistics.  The Applied Linguistics faculty coordinate and teach the courses for two undergraduate TESOL certificates. In addition, they teach courses in the ENGL BA, ENGL BA with secondary English teaching certification, ENGL MA, Elementary Education BS, and BIEM ESL Minor. The TESOL certificates are pursued by students from across the university with an enrollment of 50+ students. Texas A&M University–Corpus Christi is a vibrant, Hispanic-Serving Doctoral Research Institution that proudly provides a solid academic reputation, renowned faculty, and highly rated degree programs since 1947. The University has a heritage of teaching excellence with innovation in research and community engagement as part of the distinguished Texas A&M System. With palm tree-lined pathways throughout the campus, nearby natural wetlands, a scenic hike-and-bike trail, and a university beach, Texas A&M University–Corpus Christi is the only university in the nation located on its own island, at the heart of the Texas Gulf Coast.   Required Qualifications: A PhD in Applied Linguistics or related field. Advanced ABD will be considered; degree must be conferred at the time of appointment.    Demonstrated a successful research agenda specializing in at least two of the following areas: TESOL methods, technology-assisted language learning/teaching, corpus linguistics, or an area of sociolinguistics. Preferred qualifications: Demonstrated commitment to teaching a broad range of applied linguistics courses at the undergraduate level. Experience teaching language learners. Experience working with first-generation students and historically underserved communities. TO APPLY : https://www.tamucc.edu/human-resources/careers/index.php All required documentation must be submitted to be considered for the position. A completed application will include: A letter of interest consisting of three distinct parts: (Part 1) Directly addressing the job description; (Part 2) Explicitly presenting a teaching philosophy; and (Part 3) Providing a research statement. A current curriculum vitae Unofficial copies of graduate transcripts Three letters of recommendations Application deadline: Friday , October 23rd, 2026 Position start date: Fall 2027 For questions about the position, please contact Dr. Christopher Andrews, Department Chair, at christopher.andrews@tamucc.edu . NOTE: Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Housekeeper--Islander-Housing_R-097123-2": {
      "title": "Housekeeper, Islander Housing",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nJob Title Housekeeper, Islander Housing Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department Housing Maintenance Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Staff Job Description TAMU-CC is a dynamic university designated as both a Hispanic-Serving Institution (HSI) and Minority-Serving Institution (MSI) with approximately 11,000 students from 47 states and 54 foreign nations. We employ over 1,400 full-time and 2,000 part-time Islanders (including students/GAs). The University attracts highly talented faculty and staff and offers an array of undergraduate and graduate degrees, including doctoral programs.  As a member of the Texas A&M University System, TAMU-CC benefits from a range of resources, increased visibility and influence, and opportunities to collaborate in mutually beneficial ways with peers across member institutions and associated agencies.   TAMU-CC’s beautiful campus is located on a 240-acre island on Corpus Christi Bay and was ranked #1 College by the Sea by Best College Reviews. Our natural setting is enhanced by its modern, attractive, and state-of-the-art classroom buildings and support facilities.   PURPOSE We are seeking a Housekeeper whose responsibilities include, but are not limited to, cleaning designated areas assigned by the Maintenance Supervisor by performing the following duties: vacuuming carpet areas including resident rooms, hallways, and common areas; mopping and cleaning tile areas including resident rooms and common areas; emptying trash in their designated area; and cleaning bathrooms including tub, shower, and toilet areas.  RESPONSIBILITIES Custodial - 60% Maintaining the cleanliness of assigned building and rooms. Performs the following duties: vacuuming carpet areas including resident rooms, hallways, and common areas; mopping and cleaning tile areas including resident rooms and common areas; emptying trash in their designated area; and cleaning bathrooms including tub, shower, and toilet areas. Seeks out areas requiring cleaning; takes initiative to complete the task. Restocking custodial supplies. Contributes to the team; exhibits professionalism with students, fellow employees and others. Support - 30% Adheres to established procedures to provide a safe working environment. Reports maintenance concerns via work order requests. Other - 10% Performs other related duties as requested and assigned. QUALIFICATIONS High School diploma or GED Ability to communicate with clear written and verbal messages Ability to present a professional image Knowledge of safety equipment usage (e.g. AED, fire extinguishers, etc.), proper cleaning methods, and safety measures essential to the work environment Ability to work outdoors in temperature extremes, work with high noise levels, and must have the ability to repetitively lift up to 50 lbs. TAMU-CC Housing maintains a drug-free workplace. Ability to maintain the security and integrity of critical infrastructure, which may include communications systems, computer networks and systems, cybersecurity systems, electrical grid, hazardous waste treatment system or water treatment system. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Previous professional cleaning experience is preferred Previous housekeeping experience in residence life or college housing SALARY: up to $15.50/hourly up to $32,240/annually, approximately NOTE: Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. BENEFITS (rules, policies, eligibility apply) From our generous benefits package and professional development opportunities, to our retirement programs and our commitment to service excellence, the Island University is an engaging and rewarding place to work. Insurance Medical $0 - $30 per month for Employee Only coverage after university contribution ($920 value). Up to 83% of premium covered by the university: Employee and Spouse Employee and Children Employee and Family coverage Dental & Vision Life Insurance, Accidental D&D, Long Term Disability, Flexible Spending Account and Day Care Spending Account On-campus wellness opportunities Work Life Solutions: Counseling, Work Life Assistance, Financial Resources, and Legal Resources Tuition Benefits: Public Loan Forgiveness Book scholarships 100% tuition coverage for up to 18 credit hours per fiscal year (other rules may apply). Retirement Teacher Retirement System of Texas (TRS) or Optional Retirement Plan (ORP) Voluntary Tax Deferred Account/Deferred Compensation Plan Time Off: 8+ hours of vacation paid time off every month. 8 hours of sick leave time off every month. 8 hours of paid time off for Birthday leave. 12-15 paid holidays each year. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Assistant-Clinical-Professor--Counseling_R-097091-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Clinical Professor, Counseling",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-16\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nJob Title Assistant Clinical Professor, Counseling Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department COEHD-Counseling Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Faculty Job Description The Department of Counseling and Educational Psychology invites applications for a non-tenure track position at the rank of Assistant Clinical Professor of Counseling to begin as early as Spring 2027 . The primary responsibility of the clinical assistant professor will be to serve as director of the department’s on-campus counseling and training clinic. Additionally, this individual will be expected to engage in community outreach initiatives, teach practicum/internship; advise students; and participate in program development. The department offers CACREP-accredited master’s level programs in clinical mental health counseling; marriage, couple, and family counseling; school counseling; addictions counseling; and a doctoral program in Counselor Education. In 2020, the department was named the SACES Outstanding Master’s Level Program.  Texas A&M University-Corpus Christi is a vibrant, Hispanic and Minority Serving Doctoral Research Institution that proudly provides a solid academic reputation, renowned faculty, and highly rated degree programs since 1947. The University has a heritage of teaching excellence with innovation in research and community engagement as part of the distinguished Texas A&M System. TAMU-CC is the only university in the nation located on its own island, at the heart of the Texas Gulf Coast. With palm tree-lined pathways throughout the campus, nearby natural wetlands, a scenic hike-and-bike trail, and pristine views of the beach and bays, Texas A&M University-Corpus Christi is a first-choice institution. For more information about the Educational Leadership department, please visit: https://www.tamucc.edu/education/departments/edld/index.php Required Qualifications : A doctorate in counselor education. Licensure or licensure-eligibility as a Texas professional counselor (LPC) or marriage and family therapist (LMFT) at time of employment. Three (3) or more years of post-graduate experience as a professional counselor. Clinical supervision experience. Professional identity, experience, and training in one of the counseling specialty areas offered by the department. Experience with community engagement and outreach initiatives. Preferred Qualifications : A doctorate in counselor education from a CACREP-accredited program. Conversant in both English and Spanish. Experience attracting extramural funding. Membership and involvement in professional counseling organizations. Graduate teaching experience. Credentialed as an LPC-supervisor. Experience working with varied student populations. TO APPLY:  https://www.tamucc.edu/human-resources/careers/index.php All required documentation must be submitted to be considered for the position. A completed application will include: A cover letter that addresses the qualifications listed. A curriculum vitae. A statement of applicant’s approach to counseling and supervision. Copies of unofficial transcripts for graduate coursework. A list of three (3) professional references, including name, address, title, telephone number, and email address. The deadline for applications is Wednesday, 10/14/2026. For more information, please contact the search chair, Dr. Michelle Hollenbaugh , at Michelle.Hollenbaugh@tamucc.edu All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Senior-Director-of-Foundation-and-Corporate-Relations_R-097185-1": {
      "title": "Senior Director of Foundation and Corporate Relations",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nJob Title Senior Director of Foundation and Corporate Relations Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department Development Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Staff Job Description TAMU-CC is a dynamic university designated as both a Hispanic-Serving Institution (HSI) and Minority-Serving Institution (MSI) with approximately 11,000 students from 47 states and 54 foreign nations. We employ over 1,400 full-time and 2,000 part-time Islanders (including students/GAs). The University attracts highly talented faculty and staff and offers an array of undergraduate and graduate degrees, including doctoral programs.  As a member of the Texas A&M University System, TAMU-CC benefits from a range of resources, increased visibility and influence, and opportunities to collaborate in mutually beneficial ways with peers across member institutions and associated agencies.   TAMU-CC’s beautiful campus is located on a 240-acre island on Corpus Christi Bay and was ranked #1 College by the Sea by Best College Reviews. Our natural setting is enhanced by its modern, attractive, and state-of-the-art classroom buildings and support facilities. PURPOSE Under the direction of the Executive Associate Vice President of Development, the Senior Director of Foundation and Corporate Relations provides strategic leadership for the University's foundation and corporate fundraising program. This role is responsible for setting portfolio strategy, cultivating and closing principal- and major-level gifts and partnerships, and supervising a Foundation and Corporate Relations (FCR) Officer in identifying, cultivating, soliciting, and stewarding gifts and partnerships that advance the imperatives outlined in the University's strategic plan.   DESCRIPTION Functional Area 1: Development Percent Effort: 40% Direct research efforts to identify funding opportunities that align with the strategic direction of the University Cultivate and manage relationships with the University's most significant foundation and corporate partners, including principal-level prospects Lead the development of complex grant applications and funding proposals in support of scholarships, programs, outreach, and other work of the University's students, faculty, and staff Manage the University's fundraising request form and serve as the central point of coordination for internal campus requests to solicit funds from external foundations and corporations Advise campus partners on appropriate funders, timing, and proposal strategy prior to approving external solicitation requests Oversee stewardship strategy, ensuring accurate, timely, and compelling impact reporting on the use of funds Serve as the primary liaison between the Colleges and Institutional Advancement on foundation and corporate matters Represent the University's foundation and corporate relations function within professional networks, staying ahead of industry best practices and funding trends Functional Area 2: Strategic Leadership and Supervision Percent Effort: 30% Set the vision, strategy, and annual goals for the University's foundation and corporate relations program in alignment with institutional and divisional priorities Supervise, mentor, and develop the FCR Officer, providing guidance on prospect strategy, proposal development, and portfolio management Establish and monitor performance metrics and fundraising targets for the FCR program Manage the FCR program's operating budget and resource allocation Functional Area 3: Engagement Percent Effort: 20% Represent Institutional Advancement at high-visibility University and community events Identify and cultivate students, alumni, and donors who can help communicate the University's story and impact to corporate and foundation audiences Build and maintain a presence in key community and industry organizations, representing the University in alignment with institutional priorities Functional Area 4: Administrative Percent Effort: 10% Maintain oversight of the FCR program's portfolio of corporate and foundation donors and partners, ensuring consistent, accurate documentation of engagement Ensure timely and accurate recording of donor and prospect contacts Report quarterly on fundraising activity, pipeline health, and other metrics Maintain confidentiality at all times Perform other duties as assigned QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor's Degree Five (5) years of related experience, including demonstrated success securing major grants from foundations and corporations Additional education may be considered as a substitution for the minimum qualification requirements: Master’s degree and three (3) years of related experience, including demonstrated success securing major grants from foundations and corporations Excellent interpersonal skills with the ability to build and maintain relationships with donors, prospective donors, and campus partners at all levels Excellent verbal and written communication skills, including advanced expertise in formal proposal and grant writing Ability to relate well to coworkers, peers, and leadership across Institutional Advancement and the University as a whole Positive, collaborative, and service-oriented attitude PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Master's degree in a related field Seven to ten (7-10) years of progressive experience in foundation and corporate fundraising Prior experience supervising or mentoring staff Demonstrated track record securing six- and seven-figure gifts from foundations and corporations Experience at a university or comparable complex nonprofit Raiser's Edge experience CFRE or other relevant fundraising credential Passionate about higher education SALARY: $75,000 - $80,000/annually, approximately $6,250 - $6,666.67/monthly NOTE: Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. This position is primarily office-based with standard business hours. Some evening and weekend availability may be required to support donor events and campaign activities. The role requires the ability to sit for extended periods, operate standard office equipment, and occasionally lift materials up to 25 pounds for event setup. BENEFITS (rules, policies, eligibility apply) From our generous benefits package and professional development opportunities, to our retirement programs and our commitment to service excellence, the Island University is an engaging and rewarding place to work. Insurance Medical $0 - $30 per month for Employee Only coverage after university contribution ($920 value). Up to 83% of premium covered by the university: Employee and Spouse Employee and Children Employee and Family coverage Dental & Vision Life Insurance, Accidental D&D, Long Term Disability, Flexible Spending Account and Day Care Spending Account On-campus wellness opportunities Work Life Solutions: Counseling, Work Life Assistance, Financial Resources, and Legal Resources Tuition Benefits: Public Loan Forgiveness Book scholarships 100% tuition coverage for up to 18 credit hours per fiscal year (other rules may apply). Retirement Teacher Retirement System of Texas (TRS) or Optional Retirement Plan (ORP) Voluntary Tax Deferred Account/Deferred Compensation Plan Time Off: 8+ hours of vacation paid time off every month. 8 hours of sick leave time off every month. 8 hours of paid time off for Birthday leave. 12-15 paid holidays each year. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Academic-Success-Coach-I_R-097144-1": {
      "title": "Academic Success Coach I",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nJob Title Academic Success Coach I Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department College Readiness Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Staff Job Description TAMU-CC is a dynamic university designated as both a Hispanic-Serving Institution (HSI) and Minority-Serving Institution (MSI) with approximately 11,000 students from 47 states and 54 foreign nations. We employ over 1,400 full-time and 2,000 part-time Islanders (including students/GAs). The University attracts highly talented faculty and staff and offers an array of undergraduate and graduate degrees, including doctoral programs.  As a member of the Texas A&M University System, TAMU-CC benefits from a range of resources, increased visibility and influence, and opportunities to collaborate in mutually beneficial ways with peers across member institutions and associated agencies.   TAMU-CC’s beautiful campus is located on a 240-acre island on Corpus Christi Bay and was ranked #1 College by the Sea by Best College Reviews. Our natural setting is enhanced by its modern, attractive, and state-of-the-art classroom buildings and support facilities. PURPOSE The Academic Success Coach I, under supervision of the Academic Coaching Program Coordinator, addresses individual academic, financial, career, and social issues that affect the success of undergraduate students who are conditionally admitted to the University, as well as students who fall below good academic standing. The Academic Success Coach I connects students to resources that will assist in their success, monitors student progress, maintains records for their assigned caseload, and contributes to the overall efforts of the Academic Coaching Program. RESPONSIBILITIES Student Support: 55% Provide scholastic direction and advice to students in individual and group formats. Maintain individual caseload of students. Meet and follow up with students regarding general scholastic questions, concerns, and needs. Refer students to University resources to assist with time management, test preparation, study skills, and additional supplemental academic support services. Foster retention and identified groups of students through activities such as academic coaching, mentoring of student academic progress to include midterm and final grades, and other support programs. Program Support : 20% Assist with implementation of projects to achieve specific, academic objectives or outcomes. Respond to inquires from students and interpret university and college policies and procedures. Participate in program, unit, and campus-wide events (orientations, workshops, fairs) to advance the work of the Academic Coaching Program. Records Management: 20% Engage in related academic activities including, but not limited to, maintaining records, administering and interpreting student assessment inventories, making referrals, and managing student progress reports. Maintain records of student contracts and achievements.  Other Duties : 5% Collaborate with university staff. Participate in student recruitment and student engagement efforts. Perform other duties as assigned. QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor’s degree. Experience with at-risk populations, conditionally admitted students, and/or students on university scholastic probation. Intermediate skills in Microsoft Office Suite. Strong verbal and written communication skills. Ability to occasionally work evenings and weekends to support student success programming and university events. Familiarity with student information systems, including Navigate, Banner, DegreeWorks, and Argos. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Master’s degree. At least three (3) years’ experience with at-risk populations, academic support, and students on university scholastic probation. Experience working in higher education. SALARY: Up to $18.79 Hourly ($39,083.20 Annual, Approximately) NOTE: Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. BENEFITS (rules, policies, eligibility apply) From our generous benefits package and professional development opportunities, to our retirement programs and our commitment to service excellence, the Island University is an engaging and rewarding place to work. INSURANCE: Medical $0 - $30 per month for Employee Only coverage after university contribution ($920 value). Up to 83% of premium covered by the university: Employee and Spouse Employee and Children Employee and Family coverage Dental & Vision Life Insurance, Accidental D&D, Long Term Disability, Flexible Spending Account and Day Care Spending Account DUAL CAREER PARTNER PROGRAM ON-CAMPUS WELLNESS OPPORTUNITIES Work Life Solutions: Counseling, Work Life Assistance, Financial Resources, and Legal Resources Tuition Benefits: Public Loan Forgiveness Book scholarships 100% tuition coverage for up to 18 credit hours per fiscal year (other rules may apply). Retirement : Teacher Retirement System of Texas (TRS) or Optional Retirement Plan (ORP) Voluntary Tax Deferred Account/Deferred Compensation Plan Time Off : 8+ hours of vacation paid time off every month. 8 hours of sick leave time off every month. 8 hours of paid time off for Birthday leave. 12-15 paid holidays each year. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Visiting-Assistant-Professor-of-Political-Science_R-097134-1": {
      "title": "Visiting Assistant Professor of Political Science",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nJob Title Visiting Assistant Professor of Political Science Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department College Of Liberal Arts Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Faculty Job Description The College of Liberal Arts is seeking a Visiting Assistant Professor of Political Science in American Politics to begin in Fall 2027. The successful candidate will show excellent and enthusiastic teaching skills and demonstrate the ability to work in a team environment. Course assignments will be undergraduate courses across the Political Science curriculum. Teaching assignments may include classroom (face-to-face), hybrid, and online classes with various class sizes. Candidates should be prepared to teach core curriculum requirements such as American government and state and local government (emphasis on Texas). In addition, candidates will contribute to the teaching needs of the Political Science major including courses such as introduction to political science, Mexican American politics, research methods, and the senior capstone. The normal teaching load will be 4-4. The Department of Social Sciences (DSOC) is a multi-disciplinary department in the College of Liberal Arts that offers undergraduate degrees in Political Science and Criminal Justice and the graduate degree of Master of Public Administration. Through its contribution to the core curriculum, Political Science is one of the largest generators of student credit hours for the university. The Criminal Justice program is one of the largest undergraduate majors on campus. The MPA program is the second largest graduate program in the College of Liberal Arts. DSOC faculty are leaders on campus, in the community, and in their disciplines. We value excellence and work daily to support the University’s Islander Impact 2030 Overarching Goal to be nationally recognized for student success, academic achievement, research, creative activity, innovation, and service excellence. Texas A&M University-Corpus Christi is a vibrant, Hispanic and Minority Serving R2 Doctoral Research Institution that proudly provides a solid academic reputation, renowned faculty, and highly rated degree programs since 1947. The University has a heritage of teaching excellence with innovation in research and community engagement as part of the distinguished Texas A&M System. TAMU-CC is the only university in the nation located on its own island, at the heart of the Texas Gulf Coast. With palm tree lined pathways throughout the campus, nearby natural wetlands, a scenic hike-and-bike trail and pristine views of the beach and bays, Texas A&M University-Corpus Christi is a first-choice institution. Required Qualifications: Doctoral degree in Political Science or a closely related discipline from an accredited university. Advanced ABD will be considered; degree must be conferred at the time of appointment.  Ability to teach courses in the Political Science program (including American government, state/Texas and local government, introduction to political science, research methods, and senior capstone).  Ability to teach in a variety of formats and class sizes to include classroom, hybrid, and online courses. Commitment to excellence in teaching and student mentoring. TO APPLY : https://www.tamucc.edu/human-resources/careers/index.php All required documentation must be submitted to be considered for the position. A completed application will include: A letter of application addressing qualifications listed above including a teaching philosophy and/or evidence of excellence in teaching A curriculum vitae Three professional references including name, address, title, telephone number, and email addresses. Unofficial copies of graduate transcripts Application deadline: Sunday, November 1st, 2026 Position start date: Fall 2027 For questions about the position, please contact Dr. Isla Schuchs Carr, Department Chair, at isla.schuchscarr@tamucc.edu . NOTE: Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Assistant-Professor-of-Criminal-Justice_R-097132-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Criminal Justice",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nJob Title Assistant Professor of Criminal Justice Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department College Of Liberal Arts Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Faculty Job Description The College of Liberal Arts at Texas A&M University-Corpus Christi invites applications for a 9-month, tenure-track Assistant Professor of Criminal Justice to begin in Fall 2027. The successful candidate will demonstrate excellence in teaching, research, and service. The course load is 3-3 per academic year with research and service expectations commensurate with tenure-track appointments in the College of Liberal Arts. Course assignments will be undergraduate courses across the Criminal Justice curriculum. Teaching assignments may include classroom (face-to-face), hybrid, and online classes.   The area of specialization is open; however, candidates should be prepared to teach major requirements such as introductory classes in corrections, courts, and law enforcement as well as introduction to criminal justice, criminology, and research methods.  There may also be a need to deliver courses as part of the newly established Forensic Investigations program, an interdisciplinary minor that includes coursework from criminal justice, biomedical sciences, and psychology.  The successful candidate will demonstrate the ability to work in a team environment. The Department of Social Sciences (DSOC) is a multi-disciplinary department in the College of Liberal Arts that offers undergraduate degrees in Political Science and Criminal Justice and the graduate degree of Master of Public Administration. Through its contribution to the core curriculum, Political Science is one of the largest generators of student credit hours for the university. The Criminal Justice program is one of the largest undergraduate majors on campus. The MPA program is the second largest graduate program in the College of Liberal Arts. DSOC faculty are leaders on campus, in the community, and in their disciplines. We value excellence and work daily to support the University’s Islander Impact 2030 Overarching Goal to be nationally recognized for student success, academic achievement, research, creative activity, innovation, and service excellence. Texas A&M University-Corpus Christi is a vibrant, Hispanic and Minority Serving R2 Doctoral Research Institution that proudly provides a solid academic reputation, renowned faculty, and highly rated degree programs since 1947. The University has a heritage of teaching excellence with innovation in research and community engagement as part of the distinguished Texas A&M System. TAMU-CC is the only university in the nation located on its own island, at the heart of the Texas Gulf Coast. With palm tree lined pathways throughout the campus, nearby natural wetlands, a scenic hike-and-bike trail and pristine views of the beach and bays, Texas A&M University-Corpus Christi is a first-choice institution. Required Qualifications: Doctoral degree in Criminal Justice, Criminology, or a closely related discipline from an accredited university . Advanced ABD will be considered; degree must be conferred at the time of appointment.   Demonstrated evidence of an active research agenda that has the potential to result in published scholarly works or peer-reviewed products. Ability to teach in a variety of formats to include classroom, hybrid, and online courses. Commitment to excellence in teaching and student mentoring. Ability to teach in the core of the Criminal Justice program, including courses such as introductory classes in courts, corrections, and law enforcement as well as introduction to criminal justice, criminology, and research methods. TO APPLY : https://www.tamucc.edu/human-resources/careers/index.php All required documentation must be submitted to be considered for the position. A completed application will include: A letter of application addressing qualifications listed above including a teaching philosophy and/or evidence of excellence in teaching and a research agenda. A curriculum vitae. Three professional references including name, address, title, telephone number, and email addresses, Unofficial copies of graduate transcripts. Application deadline: Thursday, October 15th, 2026 Position start date: Fall 2027 For questions about the position, please contact Dr. Sarah Scott, Search Chair, at sarah.scott@tamucc.edu .   NOTE: Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025, and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Administrative-Associate-IV_R-097211-1": {
      "title": "Administrative Associate IV",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nJob Title Administrative Associate IV Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department College Of Liberal Arts Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Staff Job Description TAMU-CC is a dynamic university designated as both a Hispanic-Serving Institution (HSI) and Minority-Serving Institution (MSI) with approximately 11,000 students from 47 states and 54 foreign nations. We employ over 1,400 full-time and 2,000 part-time Islanders (including students/GAs). The University attracts highly talented faculty and staff and offers an array of undergraduate and graduate degrees, including doctoral programs.  As a member of the Texas A&M University System, TAMU-CC benefits from a range of resources, increased visibility and influence, and opportunities to collaborate in mutually beneficial ways with peers across member institutions and associated agencies.   TAMU-CC’s beautiful campus is located on a 240-acre island on Corpus Christi Bay and was ranked #1 College by the Sea by Best College Reviews. Our natural setting is enhanced by its modern, attractive, and state-of-the-art classroom buildings and support facilities. PURPOSE This position is designed to contribute to a collaborative administrative team that provides consistent coverage and high-quality service to the College of Liberal Arts. Responsibilities are shared and rotated among administrative associates to facilitate cross-training, maintain operational flexibility, and promote balanced workloads.   DESCRIPTION Functional Area 1: Academic & Faculty Support Percent Effort: 25% Provides administrative support to the Graduate Coordinator by maintaining application files, practicum logs, oral examination reports, and other graduate program documentation. Faculty administrative communications including missed class day postings Academic/administrative deadline tracking Other academic administrative processes as assigned Assists with adjunct hiring Assist with faculty domestic travel   Functional Area 2: Events, Travel & Operations Percent Effort: 25% Assist with the coordination of events and special events Assist with faculty foreign travel/Guest travel/Emburse Schedule rooms in Astra for special events and classes Submit work orders for maintenance Schedule meetings and maintain calendars for the college and shared conference room Hire, train, evaluate, and supervise department student workers   Functional Area 3: Financial Support Percent Effort:25% Assist with department fiscal activities, such as purchasing, Pcard, and reconciling accounts using advanced spreadsheets and/or databases. Set up vendors for payment using Substitute W9, Quote for Services, and entering Purchase Orders in IslanderBuy Oversees office supply ordering Assists with account activity/balance tracking for departments Assists in processing student awards and tracking account balances Assists with student travel/Emburse   Functional Area 4: Shared responsibilities Percent Effort:25% Front-line point of contact for the college to include greeting and answering calls Assists with general inbox coverage and general administrative requests Assists with syllabi review Assists with Interfolio Provides cross-coverage for various administrative support tasks   QUALIFICATIONS High School Diploma or GED Four (4) years of related experience Advanced skills in Microsoft Office Suite Excellent written and verbal communication skills Positive attitude Ability to work independently and efficiently with minimal supervision Ability to work in a fast-paced environment undefined PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Two (2) years of office management experience SALARY: Up to $17.50/Hourly Up to $36,400/annually, approximately NOTE: Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. BENEFITS (rules, policies, eligibility apply) From our generous benefits package and professional development opportunities, to our retirement programs and our commitment to service excellence, the Island University is an engaging and rewarding place to work. INSURANCE: Medical $0 - $30 per month for Employee Only coverage after university contribution ($920 value). Up to 83% of premium covered by the university: Employee and Spouse Employee and Children Employee and Family coverage Dental & Vision Life Insurance, Accidental D&D, Long Term Disability, Flexible Spending Account and Day Care Spending Account DUAL CAREER PARTNER PROGRAM ON-CAMPUS WELLNESS OPPORTUNITIES Work Life Solutions: Counseling, Work Life Assistance, Financial Resources, and Legal Resources Tuition Benefits: Public Loan Forgiveness Book scholarships 100% tuition coverage for up to 18 credit hours per fiscal year (other rules may apply). Retirement : Teacher Retirement System of Texas (TRS) or Optional Retirement Plan (ORP) Voluntary Tax Deferred Account/Deferred Compensation Plan Time Off : 8+ hours of vacation paid time off every month. 8 hours of sick leave time off every month. 8 hours of paid time off for Birthday leave. 12-15 paid holidays each year. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Activity-Coordinator---Programming_R-097353-1": {
      "title": "Activity Coordinator - Programming",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-23\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nJob Title Activity Coordinator - Programming Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department Student Life/Student Activities Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Staff Job Description TAMU-CC is a dynamic university designated as both a Hispanic-Serving Institution (HSI) and Minority-Serving Institution (MSI) with approximately 11,000 students from 47 states and 54 foreign nations. We employ over 1,400 full-time and 2,000 part-time Islanders (including students/GAs). The University attracts highly talented faculty and staff and offers an array of undergraduate and graduate degrees, including doctoral programs.  As a member of the Texas A&M University System, TAMU-CC benefits from a range of resources, increased visibility and influence, and opportunities to collaborate in mutually beneficial ways with peers across member institutions and associated agencies.   TAMU-CC’s beautiful campus is located on a 240-acre island on Corpus Christi Bay and was ranked #1 College by the Sea by Best College Reviews. Our natural setting is enhanced by its modern, attractive, and state-of-the-art classroom buildings and support facilities. PURPOSE  As part of our Student Activities professional team, the Coordinator for Programming will advise the Campus Activities Board (CAB) that hosts both large- and small-scale campus events, and coordinate Anchor Camp, TAMU-CC’s 3-day extended orientation camp. This position will assist with large-scale campus programming efforts such as concerts, welcome weeks, and Family Weekend events. The Coordinator supervises student leaders, builds a sense of campus community through supporting and growing campus traditions, and is an active part of the Student Activities team.     RESPONSIBILITIES  Campus Programming - 60%   Serve as the advisor for the Campus Activities Board (CAB) by providing leadership, training and support for the programming board student leaders and members. Plan and attend meetings, events, retreats, and social activities. Oversee event co-sponsorship processes, budget and risk management. Collaborate with the officers to set the vision, and goals for CAB to engage students each year.  Oversee the selection, supervision and training of CAB board members each year, and develop membership programs that support growth of CAB programs and develop pools of upcoming leaders.  Provide programmatic support and leadership on large-scale collaborative campus / division events such as concert collaborations, Fall Beach Bash, Family Weekend, Islander Lights and Homecoming.      Advising Anchor Camp - 25%   Coordinate Anchor Camp first-year camp program. Provide leadership and directions for camp, ensuring the goals of camp coincide with the mission of the department and the university. Oversee the development of camp curriculum including social, educational, wellbeing and campus spirit / tradition components.  Recruits and selects student program assistants and volunteer student staff. Develop a training plan for student staff. Work in conjunction with camp facilities on logistical needs and risk management.    Administrative, Marketing & Team Duties - 15%   Coordinate all marketing and social media for CAB, Anchor Camp and team events, with assistance of the Student Activities marketing team.  Track budget expenditures, ie: keep expenses in line with the specified line items, follow all purchasing guidelines and keep track of all expenses.  Establish working professional relationships with University faculty and staff, especially in pertinent areas such as Event Planning, UC Operations, and Catering.  Establish goals and assessments for each area of responsibility.  Represent Student Activities, on University and Division committees such as Homecoming and Waves of Welcome.  Be an active member of the Student Activities team and support departmental and campus wide programming as needed.  Perform other duties as assigned.  QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor’s degree in related field.  One (1) year progressively responsible experience developing and implementing student activities, orientations, or similar programs/related field (one year full-time or 2 years part-time/graduate assistantship).  Leadership experience in a student organization as an undergraduate/graduate.  Excellent verbal and written communications skills to be able to establish and maintain cooperative working relationships with students, faculty, staff, administrators, and community agencies.  Demonstrated experience in planning, implementing, and evaluating small to large scale university events.  Demonstrated experience in initiating and implementing leadership training for students.  Ability to understand and interpret policies, rules, and regulations.  Capacity to multitask complex assignments in a fast-paced environment and to use technology to accomplish responsibilities.  Possess strong interpersonal, organizational, and conflict management skills.  Ability to work within budgetary limits.  Ability to work weekends and evenings.    PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS  Master’s degree in higher education or related field.  Experience with a programming board or extended orientation camp.  Proficiency to develop and implement marketing strategies for programs.  Ability to create and implement a comprehensive calendar of student-centered events.  Experience working with entertainment agents and contract negotiation.   Knowledge of risk management, experiential learning, and assessment techniques.  SALARY: $43,888/annually, approximately $3,657.34/monthly NOTE: Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. BENEFITS (rules, policies, eligibility apply) From our generous benefits package and professional development opportunities, to our retirement programs and our commitment to service excellence, the Island University is an engaging and rewarding place to work. Insurance Medical$0 - $30 per month for Employee Only coverage after university contribution ($920 value).Up to 83% of premium covered by the university:Employee and SpouseEmployee and ChildrenEmployee and Family coverage Dental & Vision Life Insurance, Accidental D&D, Long Term Disability, Flexible Spending Account and Day Care Spending Account On-campus wellness opportunities Work Life Solutions: Counseling, Work Life Assistance, Financial Resources, and Legal Resources Tuition Benefits: Public Loan Forgiveness Book scholarships 100% tuition coverage for up to 18 credit hours per fiscal year (other rules may apply). Retirement Teacher Retirement System of Texas (TRS) or Optional Retirement Plan (ORP) Voluntary Tax Deferred Account/Deferred Compensation Plan Time Off: 8+ hours of vacation paid time off every month. 8 hours of sick leave time off every month. 8 hours of paid time off for Birthday leave. 12-15 paid holidays each year. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Assistant-Professor-of-Finance-Financial-Planning_R-097424-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Finance/Financial Planning",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-23\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nJob Title Assistant Professor of Finance/Financial Planning Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department College Of Business Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Faculty Job Description The Department of Finance at Texas A&M University-Corpus Christi invites applications for a 9-month tenure track, Assistant Professor of Finance, starting in Fall 2027. This position will require a doctoral degree in Financial Planning or Finance from an AACSB-accredited institution. The Department seeks candidates whose academic preparation and teaching interests complement its programs in financial planning education, including preparation for the Certified Financial Planner™ (CFP®) examination. The successful candidate will be expected to teach primarily undergraduate and/or graduate courses that support the financial planning curriculum and prepare students for the CFP® certification examination. Teaching responsibilities may include courses in areas such as principles of financial planning, retirement planning, estate planning, tax planning, insurance and risk management, investment planning, employee benefits, and financial planning capstone. The faculty member should also be prepared to teach other finance courses as needed by the department, including but not limited to corporate finance, investments, financial institutions, personal finance, and related areas. The successful candidate must be able to teach effectively in multiple instructional modalities, including face-to-face, hybrid, online, hyflex, and other formats as assigned by the department. The candidate will also be expected to maintain an active research agenda, contribute to the scholarly mission of the department and the college, advise and mentor students, participate in curriculum development, and provide service to the department, the college, the university, the profession, and the community. Accredited in both business and accounting by AACSB International, the TAMU-CC College of Business serves a varied student body on the Corpus Christi Island Campus, the RELLIS Campus (Brazos Valley), and online.  The College's values of excellence, integrity, leadership, and sustainability create an attractive environment for faculty from various backgrounds.  Please visit https://www.tamucc.edu/business/ for more information about the College and the University.  Texas A&M University-Corpus Christi is a vibrant, Hispanic and Minority Serving R2 Doctoral Research Institution that proudly provides a solid academic reputation, renowned faculty, and highly rated degree programs since 1947. The University has a heritage of teaching excellence with innovation in research and community engagement as part of the distinguished Texas A&M System. TAMU-CC is the only university in the nation located on its own island, at the heart of the Texas Gulf Coast. Required Qualifications: Doctoral degree in Financial Planning or Finance from an AACSB-accredited institution. Advanced ABD candidates will be considered; the degree must be conferred at the time of appointment. Must have earned the CFP designation or completed all educational requirements necessary to sit for the CFP® exam, which must be passed within 2 years of employment. Preferred Qualifications: Experience teaching courses aligned with CFP® Board educational requirements Relevant finance certifications   Professional and industry experience in finance-related fields. Experience teaching online/hyflex courses Experience teaching graduate courses Engagement with student activities TO APPLY: https://www.tamucc.edu/human-resources/careers/index.php All required documentation must be submitted to be considered for the position. A completed application will include: A letter of application that clearly demonstrates the applicant’s qualifications, experience, and alignment with the requirements of the position. A current curriculum vitae A statement of research philosophy A statement of teaching philosophy Evidence of CFP® certification or transcripts documenting completion of the coursework required by the CFP Board to sit for the CFP® examination Three professional references, including name, address, title, telephone number, and email addresses Unofficial copies of transcripts of graduate coursework  At least one Official Copy of student evaluations of teaching from within the last 3 years.  Application deadline: Wednesday, October 21st, 2026 Position start date: Fall 2027 For questions about the position, contact Dr. Bland, Professor of Finance and Search Committee Chair, at Eugene.bland@tamucc.edu . NOTE: Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Project-Manager-III--CAAT_R-097330-1": {
      "title": "Project Manager III, CAAT",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-23\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nJob Title Project Manager III, CAAT Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department Autonomy Research Institute Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Staff Job Description TAMU-CC is a dynamic university designated as both a Hispanic-Serving Institution (HSI) and Minority-Serving Institution (MSI) with approximately 11,000 students from 47 states and 54 foreign nations. We employ over 1,400 full-time and 2,000 part-time Islanders (including students/GAs). The University attracts highly talented faculty and staff and offers an array of undergraduate and graduate degrees, including doctoral programs.  As a member of the Texas A&M University System, TAMU-CC benefits from a range of resources, increased visibility and influence, and opportunities to collaborate in mutually beneficial ways with peers across member institutions and associated agencies. TAMU-CC’s beautiful campus is located on a 240-acre island on Corpus Christi Bay and was ranked #1 College by the Sea by Best College Reviews. Our natural setting is enhanced by its modern, attractive, and state-of-the-art classroom buildings and support facilities. PURPOSE The Project Manager III, CAAT manages assigned projects and task orders for the Center for Advanced Aviation Technologies (CAAT) and provides project management and coordination support for other CAAT projects, programs, consortium activities, working groups, and Autonomy Research Institute (ARI) activities as directed. This position is responsible for planning, coordinating, tracking, documenting, and reporting assigned project activities across CAAT staff, ARI support offices, CAAT Consortium members, external partners, subcontractors, and public-agency stakeholders. The position supports the Project Director, CAAT, by managing assigned project scope, schedules, deliverables, action items, risks, project records, proposal inputs, statement of work support, working group facilitation, and inputs for sponsor-facing reporting. This position is based in Fort Worth, Texas.  The Project Manager III for the Center for Advanced Aviation Technologies (CAAT), operating under the general direction of the Project Director, CAAT, is responsible for managing assigned CAAT projects and task orders from initiation through execution and closeout. This role may serve as the lead Project Manager for assigned projects or provide project management and coordination support to other CAAT Project Managers on additional CAAT projects, programs, consortium activities, working groups, and ARI coordination activities as directed. This role coordinates and maintains project schedules, action item logs, project records, risk and issue trackers, deliverable trackers, meeting documentation, proposal inputs, statement of work support, working group coordination materials, and project reporting materials. This position also coordinates with ARI offices and personnel when CAAT projects require support related to finance, contracting, task order administration, research administration, operational support, or other ARI-support functions. RESPONSIBILITIES Project Management and Execution: 40% Manage assigned CAAT projects and task orders from initiation through execution and closeout. Provide project management and coordination support for other CAAT projects, programs, and activities under the direction of CAAT Project Managers or the Project Director, CAAT, as well as ARI activities that align with CAAT operational requirements. Develop and maintain project plans, schedules, task lists, milestones, deliverable trackers, risk and issue logs, action item logs, and project coordination tools for assigned projects. Coordinate project activities with CAAT personnel, CAAT Consortium members, ARI support offices, subcontractors, external partners, subject matter experts, and public-agency stakeholders to support project schedules, deliverables, dependencies, and coordination requirements. Lead and coordinate project meetings for assigned projects, including kickoff meetings, recurring project meetings, technical interchange meetings, partner coordination meetings, and closeout meetings. Track project deadlines, deliverable requirements, decisions, responsible parties, dependencies, and follow-up actions. Monitor assigned projects for schedule conflicts, scope concerns, missing inputs, overdue actions, documentation gaps, and emerging risks; escalate issues to the Project Director, CAAT, or CAAT Project Managers as appropriate. Coordinate project startup activities, including project file setup, schedule development, contact list development, kickoff preparation, action item tracking, and initial project documentation. Coordinate project closeout activities, including final records organization, deliverable verification, closeout checklists, lessons learned tracking, and final documentation support. Coordinate and manage assigned project activities in accordance with CAAT policies, procedures, Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs), templates, and reporting requirements. Coordinate with the Business Operations Manager to ensure project meeting documentation, project records, and related materials are maintained in accordance with CAAT processes. Other duties assigned in support of CAAT operations and Fort Worth-based coordination requirements. Project Controls, Documentation, Reporting, and Meeting Support: 35% Prepare project meeting agendas, meeting notes, action item summaries, decision logs, attendance records, and follow-up trackers for assigned CAAT project meetings. Participate in project meetings, technical interchange meetings, internal coordination meetings, partner meetings, and sponsor-facing meetings to document decisions, action items, risks, issues, and required follow-up. Maintain organized project records, including meeting materials, project correspondence, schedules, action item logs, risk and issue logs, deliverable trackers, status reports, technical inputs, and closeout records. Support the preparation of project status reports, internal updates, sponsor-facing reporting inputs, data calls, deliverable status updates, and project documentation packages. Support CAAT Project Managers in collecting, organizing, coordinating, and integrating project-related inputs from internal staff, ARI support offices, CAAT Consortium members, working groups, subcontractors, external partners, and subject matter experts. Assist in reviewing, compiling, formatting, and routing project deliverables, reports, briefing materials, meeting packages, and supporting documentation. Ensure project records and data are stored in approved CAAT file structures and follow established records management, naming convention, reporting systems, and document control processes. Coordinate with the Business Operations Manager regarding official records, SOP compliance, document control, meeting records, and project file organization. Support recurring project reporting by maintaining accurate and current project information, including schedules, risks, issues, deliverables, dependencies, and action items. Identify missing documentation, incomplete records, inconsistent project information, and project tracking gaps; coordinate with responsible personnel to resolve issues. Proposal, Statement of Work, Working Group, Consortium, ARI, and Matrix Support: 25% Support proposal, Request for Proposal (RFP) response, task order planning, and statement of work development activities for assigned CAAT projects or larger CAAT efforts. Develop or support project schedules, deliverable matrices, project assumptions, project coordination inputs, partner input trackers, and project planning materials for proposal or task order development. Facilitate assigned CAAT working groups, including coordinating meeting schedules, agendas, attendance, action items, member input, follow-up actions, and working group documentation. Support CAAT working group activities through coordination with working group chairs, co-chairs, Consortium members, subject matter experts, CAAT Project Managers, and the Project Director, CAAT. Assist with documenting and organizing working group feedback, technical inputs, and open items, and support the transition of working group outputs into white papers, project concepts, proposal inputs, or task order planning materials when assigned. Support CAAT Consortium meetings, consortium events, all-hands meetings, technical interchange meetings, and other CAAT-wide activities as assigned. Support coordination between CAAT project execution activities and CAAT Consortium or working group outputs as white papers, industry feedback, or technical concepts transition into project planning. Coordinate with ARI personnel when CAAT projects require ARI support. Support coordination of task order-level contracting, finance, research administration, purchasing coordination, partner inputs, documentation, and other administrative requirements in coordination with the appropriate CAAT and ARI personnel. Support project intake and startup documentation, project planning, scope clarification, and partner coordination. Coordinate with public-agency stakeholders, Texas A&M University System (TAMUS), Texas A&M University – Corpus Christi (TAMU-CC), Consortium members, subcontractors, and partners in a professional and responsive manner. Maintain project contact lists, stakeholder rosters, partner responsibility trackers, distribution lists, and coordination records for assigned projects, working groups, or CAAT activities. QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor’s degree in applicable field Seven (7) years of related experience in project management, project coordination, project controls, program coordination, research administration, sponsored project support, aviation operations, public-agency project support, technical project support, or related operational support function. Additional education may substitute for the required experience. Master’s degree in applicable field, and five (5) years of related experience in project management, project coordination, project controls, program coordination, research administration, sponsored project support, aviation operations, public-agency project support, technical project support, or related operational support function. Doctoral degree and three (3) years of related experience in project management, project coordination, project controls, program coordination, research administration, sponsored project support, aviation operations, public-agency project support, technical project support, or related operational support function. Experience supporting project schedules, action item tracking, risk and issue logs, deliverable trackers, status reports, or project dashboards. Experience supporting project closeout, lessons learned, final reports, or sponsor-facing deliverable packages. SKILLS AND ABILITIES Strong written and verbal communication skills Intermediate proficiency in Microsoft Office Suite High level of articulation and professionalism Exceptional attention to detail Critical thinking and proactive problem solving Ability to take initiative and identify opportunities for improvement Ability to either work in a team environment or independently with minimal guidance Ability to independently manage multiple projects, priorities, schedules, deliverables, risks, and follow-up while supporting other CAAT activities as directed. Ability to prepare and maintain project documentation, including meeting agendas and notes, action item logs, project trackers, deliverable trackers, and status-reporting inputs. Ability to support proposal development, RFP response preparation, statement of work development, project intake, and task order startup documentation. Ability to facilitate and support working groups, CAAT Consortium meetings, all-hands meetings, technical interchange meetings, and other multi-stakeholder activities, including coordinating agendas, tracking inputs and action items, and supporting follow-up. Ability to coordinate effectively across ARI Business Administration, Grants and Research Administration, contracting, finance, Operations, Applied Research, university offices, CAAT Consortium members, subcontractors, external partners, and public-agency stakeholders. Ability to assign, coordinate, and monitor work performed by student workers, matrix-assigned personnel, or other assigned project support personnel. Ability to identify missing information, schedule conflicts, documentation gaps, project risks, and coordination issues. Ability to work effectively within federal, state, local, university, and public-agency project environments. Ability to travel up to 20% for position-associated missions and training sessions Ability to lift 50 lbs. and occasionally work outdoors PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Master’s degree or higher in Project Management, Business Administration, Aviation, or related field. More than seven (7) years of related project management, project coordination, project controls, program coordination, public-agency project support, aviation operations, research administration, or sponsored project support experience. Project Management Certification or project management training. Experience managing projects or task orders with defined scope, schedule, deliverables, and partner coordination requirements. Experience supporting federally sponsored projects, task orders, research projects, public-agency programs, or multi-partner technical projects. Experience in aviation, advanced aviation, autonomy, transportation, public-sector technology, research operations, or university research administration. Experience supporting proposal development, RFP responses, statements of work, project planning packages, project intake, or task order startup documentation. Experience facilitating or supporting working groups, committees, technical teams, project kickoff meetings, technical interchange meetings, consortium meetings, partner meetings, or other multi-stakeholder coordination activities. Experience coordinating across business administration, contracting, finance, research or grants administration, operational support offices, university offices, CAAT Consortium members, subcontractors, external partners, and public-agency stakeholders. SALARY: $6,666.67 - $7,5000.00 Monthly ($80,000.04 - $90,000.00 Annual, Approximately) NOTE:  Required to work in Fort Worth, Texas office location. Salary is dependent upon education and experience. All ARI employees may utilize university vehicles for official business travel, including travel to the main campus, local meetings, events, training, and travel within Texas. Employees who operate university vehicles must possess and maintain a valid driver's license. Due to federal law on deemed export controls/ITAR compliance, it is not possible to petition for visa sponsorship. Applicants must be a U.S. persons. BENEFITS (rules, policies, eligibility apply) From our generous benefits package and professional development opportunities, to our retirement programs and our commitment to service excellence, the Island University is an engaging and rewarding place to work. INSURANCE: Medical $0 - $30 per month for Employee Only coverage after university contribution ($920 value). Up to 83% of premium covered by the university: Employee and Spouse Employee and Children Employee and Family coverage Dental & Vision Life Insurance, Accidental D&D, Long Term Disability, Flexible Spending Account and Day Care Spending Account DUAL CAREER PARTNER PROGRAM ON-CAMPUS WELLNESS OPPORTUNITIES Work Life Solutions: Counseling, Work Life Assistance, Financial Resources, and Legal Resources Tuition Benefits: Public Loan Forgiveness Book scholarships 100% tuition coverage for up to 18 credit hours per fiscal year (other rules may apply). Retirement : Teacher Retirement System of Texas (TRS) or Optional Retirement Plan (ORP) Voluntary Tax Deferred Account/Deferred Compensation Plan Time Off : 8+ hours of vacation paid time off every month. 8 hours of sick leave time off every month. 8 hours of paid time off for Birthday leave. 12-15 paid holidays each year. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Program-Director---Pollution-Prevention-Partnership-and-AutoCheck_R-097374-1": {
      "title": "Program Director - Pollution Prevention Partnership and AutoCheck",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-23\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nJob Title Program Director - Pollution Prevention Partnership and AutoCheck Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department Research Engagement Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Staff Job Description TAMU-CC is a dynamic university designated as both a Hispanic-Serving Institution (HSI) and Minority-Serving Institution (MSI) with approximately 11,000 students from 47 states and 54 foreign nations. We employ over 1,400 full-time and 2,000 part-time Islanders (including students/GAs). The University attracts highly talented faculty and staff and offers an array of undergraduate and graduate degrees, including doctoral programs.  As a member of the Texas A&M University System, TAMU-CC benefits from a range of resources, increased visibility and influence, and opportunities to collaborate in mutually beneficial ways with peers across member institutions and associated agencies.   TAMU-CC’s beautiful campus is located on a 240-acre island on Corpus Christi Bay and was ranked #1 College by the Sea by Best College Reviews. Our natural setting is enhanced by its modern, attractive, and state-of-the-art classroom buildings and support facilities. PURPOSE The Program Director for Pollution Prevention Partnership and AutoCheck, under general direction, directs the administration of assigned programs, including planning, organizing, staffing, leading, and controlling program activities. The position directs the Pollution Prevention Partnership and AutoCheck program; supports the research community through direct engagement with faculty and staff,  maintains program websites; and helps support basic information technology resources with the team. RESPONSIBILITIES Program Administration: 90% Establishes strategic goals and designs innovative solutions to local and regional air quality issues. Develops and maintains cooperative relationships with external stakeholders of the Pollution Prevention Partnership and AutoCheck program. Writes program applications, work plans and reports. Researches and identifies possible funding sources. Writes grants and responds to Requests for Proposals as needed. Develops work plans to meet funding source needs and sustain/grow program. Produces accurate monthly reports to funding sources, contract representatives, and University staff. Reports will include activities and deliverables performed, environmental impacts , income and expense status of program, and budgeting information. Recruits, hires and trains student workers and interns to assist in program operations. Coordinates and delivers AutoCheck 'Clean Fleet' program. Conducts program emission testing sessions in outdoor environments. Organizes, sets up, and breaks down testing events. Gathers emissions data, compiles and submits accurate and timely reports. Markets and recruits for program partners and stakeholders to participate in program. Meets with community members and officials and encourages their participation in program. Ensures program website is current, produces accurate and timely reports. Researches emerging technologies related to pollution prevention. Evaluates, purchases, maintains, upgrades, and operates equipment and software. Fulfills all terms of program contract with Port of Corpus Christi and other funding partners. Ensures continued eligibility for repair voucher funding to support the AutoCheck program. Research/Program Support: 5% Supports Research Engagement initiatives to help increase research funding. Provides consultation to faculty and staff in the proposal development process. Develops and supports organizational research impact capacity. Other: 5% Helps to maintain organizational websites and information technology resources among team. Safely operates university vehicles/machinery. Other duties as assigned. QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor’s degree in applicable field. Ten (10) years of related experience Additional education may be considered as a substitution for the minimum experience requirements: Master’s degree in applicable field, and eight (8) years of related experience; OR Doctoral degree in applicable field, and six (6) years of related experience. Maintains a valid driver’s license. Experience in program administration, environmental or community engagement programming, grants and contracts, research support, sponsored projects, or related program operations. Experience developing, administering, and reporting on program activities, work plans, deliverables, budgets, and funding-source requirements. Experience writing grants, reports, work plans, or responses to Requests for Proposals. Experience working with external stakeholders, program partners, funding sources, contract representatives, university staff, faculty, or community members. Experience coordinating program activities, outreach, data collection, reporting, and event logistics. Experience supporting research engagement, proposal development, or sponsored project activities. Experience maintaining program websites or supporting basic information technology resources in a team. SKILLS AND ABILITIES Ability and willingness to learn, adopt, and apply new technologies, software applications, databases, websites, and digital tools to support program operations, reporting, communication, and continuous improvement. Experience in environmental or community engagement programming. Familiarity with program development. Intermediate proficiency in Microsoft Office Suite, word processing, spreadsheet, and database applications. High level of articulation and professionalism. Strong interpersonal and communication skills. Exceptional attention to detail. Understanding of sponsored projects. Service Excellence proficiency. Critical thinking and proactive problem solving. Ability to multitask and work cooperatively with others. Ability to support research engagement activities and initiatives. Ability to take initiative and identify opportunities for improvement. Ability to either work in a team environment or independently with minimal guidance. Ability to manage and prioritize tasks, resources, and time effectively. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Education, training or experience in research support activities. Experience sustaining or growing externally funded programs through continuation funding, work plans, sponsor reporting, and responses to Requests for Proposals. Experience preparing environmental impact summaries, program activity reports, income and expense updates, and deliverable reports for funding sources or contract representatives. Experience designing, maintaining, or improving public-facing program websites or digital resources to support program communication and stakeholder engagement. Experience working with community members, public officials, funding partners, and external stakeholders in support of environmental or research-related programs. SALARY: COMMENSURATE ( Salary dependent upon education/experience ) NOTE: Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. BENEFITS (rules, policies, eligibility apply) From our generous benefits package and professional development opportunities, to our retirement programs and our commitment to service excellence, the Island University is an engaging and rewarding place to work. INSURANCE: Medical $0 - $30 per month for Employee Only coverage after university contribution ($920 value). Up to 83% of premium covered by the university: Employee and Spouse Employee and Children Employee and Family coverage Dental & Vision Life Insurance, Accidental D&D, Long Term Disability, Flexible Spending Account and Day Care Spending Account DUAL CAREER PARTNER PROGRAM ON-CAMPUS WELLNESS OPPORTUNITIES Work Life Solutions: Counseling, Work Life Assistance, Financial Resources, and Legal Resources Tuition Benefits: Public Loan Forgiveness Book scholarships 100% tuition coverage for up to 18 credit hours per fiscal year (other rules may apply). Retirement : Teacher Retirement System of Texas (TRS) or Optional Retirement Plan (ORP) Voluntary Tax Deferred Account/Deferred Compensation Plan Time Off : 8+ hours of vacation paid time off every month. 8 hours of sick leave time off every month. 8 hours of paid time off for Birthday leave. 12-15 paid holidays each year. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Admissions-Specialist-IV_R-097287-1": {
      "title": "Admissions Specialist IV",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-21\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Title Admissions Specialist IV Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department Office Of Enrollment Processing Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Staff Job Description TAMU-CC is a dynamic university designated as both a Hispanic-Serving Institution (HSI) and Minority-Serving Institution (MSI) with approximately 11,000 students from 47 states and 54 foreign nations. We employ over 1,400 full-time and 2,000 part-time Islanders (including students/GAs). The University attracts highly talented faculty and staff and offers an array of undergraduate and graduate degrees, including doctoral programs.  As a member of the Texas A&M University System, TAMU-CC benefits from a range of resources, increased visibility and influence, and opportunities to collaborate in mutually beneficial ways with peers across member institutions and associated agencies.   TAMU-CC’s beautiful campus is located on a 240-acre island on Corpus Christi Bay and was ranked #1 College by the Sea by Best College Reviews. Our natural setting is enhanced by its modern, attractive, and state-of-the-art classroom buildings and support facilities. PURPOSE This position supports undergraduate admissions operations through the processing and review of applications, transcripts, and other admissions documentation, as well as front-line operational support of admissions systems and workflows. The position identifies and resolves routine processing, data, and workflow issues, supports departmental users, assists with testing and documentation, and escalates system-level issues to the appropriate technical team.   DESCRIPTION Functional Area 1: Admissions Operations Percent Effort: 60% Process undergraduate applications, high school and college transcripts, test scores, and other supporting admissions documentation. Review application files and requirements to determine admissions eligibility and support issuance of admissions decisions. Review high school and college transcripts to identify and appropriately code coursework and other admissions-related information. Review error reports and admissions records to identify, research, and correct data discrepancies. Prepare admissions appeals and supporting documentation for review by the Undergraduate Admissions Committee. Assist with residency, meningitis, and other admissions-related documentation and compliance requirements. Monitor admissions processing workflows to identify operational issues that may affect application processing, data accuracy, or timely completion of admissions records. Troubleshoot and resolve routine admissions processing, data, and workflow issues within established procedures and available system functionality. Functional Area 2: Systems, Workflow, and Process Support Percent Effort: 35% Serve as a departmental resource for admissions staff regarding established CRM, SIS, imaging, application, and related system workflows. Research recurring processing or system issues and document findings for resolution. Test admissions workflows, system updates, and process changes, and document results. Assist with validating data, workflow functionality, and communication processes following system updates or changes. Maintain process documentation, procedures, and job aids related to admissions systems and workflows. Provide training and front-line support to departmental users on established admissions system processes and functionality. Identify issues requiring system configuration, development, or maintenance and escalate them to the appropriate technical or Project Management team. Support communication campaigns and other enrollment-related processes using established system tools and workflows. Provide support for recruitment, admission, and retention activities sponsored by the Office of Admissions and/or Division of Enrollment Management. Functional Area 3: Other Percent Effort: 5% Perform other duties as assigned.   QUALIFICATIONS Associate's degree in a related field. Five (5) years of related experience. Additional education may be considered as a substitution for the minimum experience requirement. Bachelor’s degree in a related field and three (3) years of related work experience. OR Master’s degree in a related field and one (1) year of related work experience. Ability to effectively communicate technical information to non-technical users verbally, in writing, and in training presentations to groups or individuals. Experience in data compilation and analysis. Experience in a higher education setting.   PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor’s or Master’s degree in Computer Science or Higher Education Administration. Experience working with Banner, Laserfiche, and Argos. Experience working in higher education, admissions, supporting admissions processes, and enrollment operations. Experience working with CRM systems, including a background in designing and implementing complex workflows and reporting solutions.     SALARY:   Up to $19.23 Hourly (Up to $39,998.40 Annually, Approximately) NOTE:   Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. BENEFITS (rules, policies, eligibility apply) From our generous benefits package and professional development opportunities, to our retirement programs and our commitment to service excellence, the Island University is an engaging and rewarding place to work. INSURANCE: Medical $0 - $30 per month for Employee Only coverage after university contribution ($920 value). Up to 83% of premium covered by the university: Employee and Spouse Employee and Children Employee and Family coverage Dental & Vision Life Insurance, Accidental D&D, Long Term Disability, Flexible Spending Account and Day Care Spending Account DUAL CAREER PARTNER PROGRAM ON-CAMPUS WELLNESS OPPORTUNITIES Work Life Solutions: Counseling, Work Life Assistance, Financial Resources, and Legal Resources Tuition Benefits: Public Loan Forgiveness Book scholarships 100% tuition coverage for up to 18 credit hours per fiscal year (other rules may apply). Retirement : Teacher Retirement System of Texas (TRS) or Optional Retirement Plan (ORP) Voluntary Tax Deferred Account/Deferred Compensation Plan Time Off : 8+ hours of vacation paid time off every month. 8 hours of sick leave time off every month. 8 hours of paid time off for Birthday leave. 12-15 paid holidays each year. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
