    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Director-of-Creative-Services_R-097346-1": {
      "title": "Director of Creative Services",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-21\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Title Director of Creative Services Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department Institutional Advancement Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Staff Job Description TAMU-CC is a dynamic university designated as both a Hispanic-Serving Institution (HSI) and Minority-Serving Institution (MSI) with approximately 11,000 students from 47 states and 54 foreign nations. We employ over 1,400 full-time and 2,000 part-time Islanders (including students/GAs). The University attracts highly talented faculty and staff and offers an array of undergraduate and graduate degrees, including doctoral programs.  As a member of the Texas A&M University System, TAMU-CC benefits from a range of resources, increased visibility and influence, and opportunities to collaborate in mutually beneficial ways with peers across member institutions and associated agencies.   TAMU-CC’s beautiful campus is located on a 240-acre island on Corpus Christi Bay and was ranked #1 College by the Sea by Best College Reviews. Our natural setting is enhanced by its modern, attractive, and state-of-the-art classroom buildings and support facilities. PURPOSE The Director of Creative Services, reporting to the Assistant Vice President of Marketing and Communications, provides strategic creative leadership and serves as the university’s lead art director and visual storyteller. The Director leads a cross-functional team of creative professionals responsible for graphic design, photography, videography, and social media, establishing the creative vision and standards that define and strengthen the Islander brand.   In collaboration with Marketing, Communications, Web Services, and campus partners, the Director translates institutional priorities and marketing strategies into compelling creative content and visual storytelling that increases visibility and engages key audiences.   DESCRIPTION Functional Area 1: Creative Direction & Visual Brand Leadership Percent Effort: 30% Establish and lead the university's long-term creative vision and visual identity across institutional campaigns, communications, platforms, and initiatives. Serve as the university's lead art director and visual storyteller, defining how the Islander brand comes to life through design, photography, videography, social media, and other visual communications. Develop, maintain, evolve, and enforce university visual brand standards and the university brand and style guide. Lead the development of creative concepts and visual approaches for institutional marketing, student recruitment, advancement, and other university priorities. Ensure creative work consistently reflects and strengthens the university's brand positioning and institutional identity. Provide creative and brand counsel to university leadership and campus partners. Present and articulate creative concepts, recommendations, and brand rationale to university leadership and stakeholders. Monitor creative, design, visual storytelling, social media, and industry trends and identify opportunities to strengthen and evolve the university's creative presence.   Functional Area 2: Integrated Creative Content & Production Percent Effort: 25% Provide strategic and artistic direction for graphic design, photography, videography, social media content, publications, advertising creative, and other university-level creative assets. Lead the creative development process from concept and creative brief through production, review, approval, and final delivery. Art direct major institutional campaigns and high-visibility projects, ensuring creative work effectively supports established marketing strategies, audiences, and objectives. Direct the visual development of major institutional and student recruitment publications and materials in collaboration with Marketing, Enrollment Management, and other campus partners. Provide direction for photo and video shoots, campaign concepts, storyboards, visual storytelling, and other multimedia productions. Ensure creative assets are compelling, audience-focused, brand-aligned, accessible, and appropriate for print, digital, social, advertising, and emerging channels. Establish and maintain high standards for creative quality and consistency across university-level work. Partner with Marketing to review creative performance and identify opportunities to optimize campaign creative based on audience response and results.   Functional Area 3: Social Media & Digital Storytelling Percent Effort: 15% Provide strategic leadership and creative direction for the university's top-tier social media presence and digital storytelling. Develop content strategies and editorial direction that showcase campus life, academic excellence, research, student success, institutional achievements, and the Islander experience. Identify opportunities to use social media and emerging platforms to increase engagement, strengthen affinity, and expand the university's reach. Establish university-wide social media policies, guidelines, governance, and best practices for decentralized university accounts. Monitor social media performance, audience engagement, content trends, sentiment, and emerging platforms to inform strategy and creative development. Establish key performance indicators and regularly evaluate the effectiveness of university social media and digital content. Collaborate with Communications and university leadership on social listening, emerging issues, and reputational considerations.   Functional Area 4: Creative Operations & Resource Management Percent Effort: 15% Oversee creative workflows, production schedules, project assignments, and resources across the creative services team. Establish processes for evaluating and prioritizing creative requests based on institutional priorities, strategic value, audience impact, available resources, and team capacity. Assign projects based on staff expertise, workload, project requirements, and institutional priorities. Establish effective creative briefs, review processes, production standards, and project management practices that support efficient and collaborative workflows. Ability to step in and perform senior-level graphic design or media production and editing work as needed during high-volume periods. Manage assigned creative services budgets and identify opportunities to maximize available resources. Oversee external creative vendors, freelancers, printers, photographers, production partners, and related contracts as needed. Evaluate emerging creative technologies, tools, platforms, and production methods that can improve quality, efficiency, and effectiveness.   Functional Area 5: Team Leadership & Campus Collaboration Percent Effort: 15% Lead, supervise, mentor, and develop professional staff responsible for graphic design, photography, videography, and social media. Establish team priorities, performance expectations, workflows, and professional development goals aligned with institutional and departmental priorities. Foster a collaborative, innovative, accountable, and high-performing creative environment. Encourage creative exploration and professional growth while maintaining high standards for quality, consistency, and service. Serve as a strategic creative resource to university leadership and the campus community. Build effective partnerships across academic and administrative units to improve coordination, planning, and effective use of university creative resources. Collaborate with Marketing leadership to align creative development with audience strategy, campaign objectives, advertising initiatives, and institutional marketing priorities. Serve on university committees, task forces, and working groups as assigned. Represent the university at professional, community, and industry events as appropriate. Perform other duties as assigned.   QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor's degree in graphic design, fine arts, communications, marketing, advertising, public relations, digital media, or a closely related field. Ten (10) years of progressively responsible professional experience in creative direction, graphic design, branding, multimedia production, marketing communications, or a related field. Additional education may be considered as a substitute for the minimum experience requirement: Master’s degree in graphic design, fine arts, communications, marketing, advertising, public relations, digital media, or a closely related field with eight (8) years of progressively responsible professional experience in creative direction, graphic design, branding, multimedia production, marketing communications, or a related field Doctoral degree in graphic design, fine arts, communications, marketing, advertising, public relations, digital media, or a closely related field with six (6) years of progressively responsible professional experience in creative direction, graphic design, branding, multimedia production, marketing communications, or a related field Demonstrated experience leading, supervising, and developing creative professionals. Demonstrated experience establishing creative direction and translating organizational or marketing strategy into compelling visual concepts and executions. Significant experience developing and maintaining visual brand identities and brand standards. Demonstrated experience directing integrated creative work across multiple disciplines, including graphic design, photography, videography, digital content, and social media. Strong understanding of print and digital production processes, creative workflows, and project management. Knowledge of major social media platforms, digital content strategy, social media analytics, and emerging content trends. Advanced knowledge of creative software and technologies, including Adobe Creative Cloud or comparable platforms. Demonstrated ability to manage multiple high-profile projects, priorities, budgets, deadlines, and stakeholders simultaneously. Strong leadership, creative problem-solving, presentation, communication, and collaboration skills. Demonstrated ability to provide creative direction, constructive feedback, and quality control while maintaining productive relationships with internal clients and creative professionals.   PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Master's degree in graphic design, fine arts, communications, marketing, advertising, or a related field. Creative leadership experience within higher education or another large, complex organization. Experience leading a multidisciplinary creative team that includes graphic design, photography, videography, and social media. Experience providing creative direction for large-scale institutional, recruitment, reputation, or integrated marketing campaigns. Experience developing and managing organizational brand and visual identity standards. Experience with digital asset management systems and creative project management platforms. Experience developing social media content strategies and overseeing institutional or organizational social media channels. SALARY: $85,000 - $95,000/annually, approximately $7,083.34 - $7,916.67/monthly NOTE: Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. BENEFITS (rules, policies, eligibility apply) From our generous benefits package and professional development opportunities, to our retirement programs and our commitment to service excellence, the Island University is an engaging and rewarding place to work. Insurance Medical$0 - $30 per month for Employee Only coverage after university contribution ($920 value).Up to 83% of premium covered by the university:Employee and SpouseEmployee and ChildrenEmployee and Family coverage Dental & Vision Life Insurance, Accidental D&D, Long Term Disability, Flexible Spending Account and Day Care Spending Account On-campus wellness opportunities Work Life Solutions: Counseling, Work Life Assistance, Financial Resources, and Legal Resources Tuition Benefits: Public Loan Forgiveness Book scholarships 100% tuition coverage for up to 18 credit hours per fiscal year (other rules may apply). Retirement Teacher Retirement System of Texas (TRS) or Optional Retirement Plan (ORP) Voluntary Tax Deferred Account/Deferred Compensation Plan Time Off: 8+ hours of vacation paid time off every month. 8 hours of sick leave time off every month. 8 hours of paid time off for Birthday leave. 12-15 paid holidays each year. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Assistant-Full-Professor-of-Clinical-Psychology_R-097139": {
      "title": "Assistant/Full Professor of Clinical Psychology",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-21\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Title Assistant/Full Professor of Clinical Psychology Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department College Of Liberal Arts Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Faculty Job Description The Department of Psychology and Sociology, at Texas A&M University-Corpus Christi, invites applications for a full-time (9-month), tenure-track position in Clinical Psychology beginning Fall of 2027. Faculty rank and tenure status will be determined upon review of qualifications. The department offers a Bachelor of Arts in Psychology in both traditional and fully online formats, as well as a Master of Arts in Clinical Psychology. We are seeking Full Professor candidates with doctoral-level program experience, as we are considering the development of a PhD in Clinical Psychology. The Psychology programs are among the largest in the College of Liberal Arts, with approximately 500 majors, 100 minors, and 40 graduate students. We seek applicants with a Ph.D. or Psy.D. in Clinical or Counseling Psychology expected to be conferred by the start of the appointment.  Applicants must be licensed or license eligible as a psychologist in Texas.  Priority will be given to candidates with research experience in clinical psychology.  Successful candidates will be expected to teach clinical psychology courses at the undergraduate and master’s level, which may include courses in psychological assessment, psychopathology, and psychotherapy. There may also be opportunities to develop courses in their area of specialization. Tenure-track faculty carry a standard 3-3 teaching load. TAMU-CC is an R2 University, and the department has an increasing focus on research.  We seek candidates with a record of (or potential for) research productivity, and an interest in pursuing external funding.  The successful candidate’s research activities will include mentoring student research and supervising master’s thesis students in our Clinical Psychology MA program. Individuals considered for this position will also be expected to provide clinical supervision to master’s level students, and actively engage in service to the department, college, university, and profession. The successful candidate should have an understanding of multifaceted  issues and relevant experience in their applications to pedagogy and clinical practice. Texas A&M University-Corpus Christi is a vibrant, Hispanic and Minority Serving R2 Doctoral Research Institution that proudly provides a solid academic reputation, renowned faculty, and highly rated degree programs since 1947. The University has a heritage of teaching excellence with innovation in research and community engagement as part of the distinguished Texas A&M System.  It is the only university in the nation located on its own island, at the heart of the Texas Gulf Coast.  With palm tree lined pathways throughout the campus, nearby natural wetlands, a scenic hike-and-bike trail and pristine views of the beach and bay, TAMU-CC is a first-choice institution. For more information on our programs visit our website at: https://www.tamucc.edu/liberal-arts/departments/psychology-sociology/index.php Required Qualifications: Assistant Rank: Earned Ph.D. or Psy.D. in Clinical Psychology or Counseling Psychology from an APA or CPA accredited program. Advanced ABD will be considered; a degree must be conferred by the time of appointment. Be license eligible as a psychologist in the state of Texas at the time of appointment. Full Professor Rank: Earned Ph.D. or Psy.D. in Clinical Psychology or Counseling Psychology from an APA or CPA accredited program. Be license eligible as a psychologist in the state of Texas at the time of appointment. To be considered for the Full Professor rank, a minimum of ten (10) years of full-time university faculty experience with a minimum of four (4) years of experience at the associate rank or comparable rank is required. Preferred Qualifications: Licensed as a psychologist, preferably in the state of Texas. Independent Clinical experience or research focused in Clinical Psychology. Experience and/or ability to teach/develop courses at the master’s and undergraduate level. A record of, or potential for, research productivity and a clear research agenda. Interest and/or experience in seeking external funding for research. A record of mentoring students in research and supervising clinical students. TO APPLY: https://www.tamucc.edu/human-resources/careers All required documentation must be submitted to be considered for the position. A complete application will include: A letter of application addressing the qualifications listed and relevant professional experience. A curriculum vitae. Unofficial transcripts of graduate coursework. A two-to-three-page statement summarizing the candidate’s teaching experience and effectiveness. A two-to-three-page statement summarizing the candidate’s recent research activities, as well as research plans for the near future. Contact information for three professional references including name, address, title, telephone number, and email address.  The job posting will remain open until a finalist has been selected. Application reviews will begin on October 6, 2026. Questions about the position should be directed to the Search Committee Chair, Dr. Miguel Moreno, at 361-825-2205 or miguel.moreno@tamucc.edu . NOTE: Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Academic-Advisor-II_R-097243-1": {
      "title": "Academic Advisor II",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-18\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Title Academic Advisor II Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department Academic Advising Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Staff Job Description TAMU-CC is a dynamic university designated as both a Hispanic-Serving Institution (HSI) and Minority-Serving Institution (MSI) with app roximately 11,000 students from 47 states and 54 foreign nations. We employ over 1,400 full-time and 2,000 part-time Islanders (including students/GAs). The University attracts highly talented faculty and staff and offers an array of undergraduate and graduate degrees, including doctoral programs.  As a member of the Texas A&M University System, TAMU-CC benefits from a range of resources, increased visibility and influence, and opportunities to collaborate in mutually beneficial ways with peers across member institutions and associated agencies. TAMU-CC’s beautiful campus is located on a 240-acre island on Corpus Christi Bay and was ranked #1 College by the Sea by Best College Reviews. Our natural setting is enhanced by its modern, attractive, and state-of-the-art classroom buildings and support facilities. PURPOSE This position under general supervision, advises students concerning the academic requirements for their chosen program and reports directly to the Senior Academic Advisor.  This position is responsible for assisting students with course selection, registration process, and major selection. This position provides information on and refers students to university resources that can assist in meeting their needs or solving problems; communicates accurate and timely information and interprets institutional, core, and major requirements. May supervise projects. RESPONSIBILITIES Academic Advising: 65% Provides academic advising for students grounded in tenets of practice outlined by the National Association of Academic Advising (NACADA) and appropriate academic advising and student development theories. Advises for course selection, registration, academic planning, career and educational goal-setting, appropriate resources, transitioning to the university, and retention issues. Document student advising outcomes adequately to meet department and institutional disclosure guidelines and aid in subsequent advising activities. Advises in both group and individual settings with current and prospective students. Perform appointment preparations including, but not limited to, reviewing all accessible academic records for accuracy and appropriateness of course work, communicating with academic departments for major-specific transfer equivalencies, and requesting the posting of approved transfer courses. Process academic forms and follow-up on processed forms to ensure that the student's record accurately represents the student's chosen major, minor, concentration, and catalog year. Interprets State, University, and college policies and procedures. Complies with all state, system, and university policies, regulations, rules, procedures, guidelines, and directives in a timely manner and adhere to confidentiality policies (FERPA). Student Success & Retention : 20% Advocate for student needs associated with their educational and academic program. Clearly communicate accurate and timely information to students and interpret institutional, general education, and major requirements per the university catalog. Exhibit strong, active listening skills. Help students develop a realistic self-perception and successful transition into the university and help them to establish realistic goals for future endeavors. Monitors academic progress and implements intervention strategies for students who receive academic alerts or who need assistance in maintaining or improving their academic standing. Direct students to resources, programs and services on campus when it is appropriate to do so.    Help students integrate information so that they may make well-informed academic decisions. Work to establish a partnership with students that will guide them efficiently through their academic program. Monitor students advising requirements: Notify students of academic advising requirements when certain conditions have or have not been met. Maintain accurate and complete student file documentation. Prepare for and participate in on- and off-campus recruiting activities. Prepare for, present and advise at first-year student orientations. Support retention efforts include using Navigate and requiring probationary and returning     suspension/enforce withdrawal students to schedule an appointment and discuss university guidelines and expectations. Leads workshops, seminars, and other student engagement activities across campus. Leads and oversees special projects specific to the academic college or advising center. Maintain a professional and cordial liaison with administrators, faculty, staff, students, and other involved constituents concerning advisees and program. Track and obtain documents needed for potential and current students from other constituencies on campus. Collaboration: 10% Collaborates with members of the campus community in student retention initiatives. Seeks opportunities to serve and contribute on appropriate committees and task forces across campus to advocate for students. Conducts research on topics within the field of academic advising and organizes activities for professional development. Attend TAMU-CC Commencement ceremonies. Reporting/Other: 5% May provide reports and data analysis of advising initiatives and special populations. Mentors and builds strong relationships with other advisors. Perform other duties as assigned. QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor’s degree. Two (2) years of experience advising students in higher education or related experience. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, & ABILITIES May travel to national and/or regional advising conferences; may be required to work beyond normal office hours including evening and/or on weekends. Intermediate skills in Microsoft Office Suite (Word, Excel, PowerPoint, and Outlook), databases, and spreadsheets. Strong active listening, written, and verbal communication skills. Ability and willingness to work as a team member and to work independently. Demonstrated initiative and follow-through skills; tact, diplomacy, and judgment in dealing with students, parents, faculty, and administrators. Detail-oriented and demonstrated ability to multi-task in a fast-paced environment. Ability to work beyond normal office hours including evening and/or weekends. Ability to lead students in developing a sense of purpose and belonging while developing their educational and personal goals. Attend and/or present at the national conference (i.e., NACADA, TEXANN, Texas Success Conference, etc.) and implemented applicable best practices across the unit Ability to use student information systems, degree planning, and degree audits. Ability to multitask and work cooperatively with others, present information clearly and concisely, work with sensitive information and maintain confidentiality. Proficient analytical, interpersonal, and organizational skills. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Three (3) years of experience advising students in higher education. Experience with Banner, Argos, Navigate 360, Laserfiche, and Degree Works. Demonstrated knowledge of developmental academic advising methodologies. SALARY: $3,657.34 Monthly ($43,888.08 Annual, Approximately) NOTE:  Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. BENEFITS (rules, policies, eligibility apply) From our generous benefits package and professional development opportunities, to our retirement programs and our commitment to service excellence, the Island University is an engaging and rewarding place to work. INSURANCE: Medical $0 - $30 per month for Employee Only coverage after university contribution ($920 value). Up to 83% of premium covered by the university: Employee and Spouse Employee and Children Employee and Family coverage Dental & Vision Life Insurance, Accidental D&D, Long Term Disability, Flexible Spending Account and Day Care Spending Account DUAL CAREER PARTNER PROGRAM ON-CAMPUS WELLNESS OPPORTUNITIES Work Life Solutions: Counseling, Work Life Assistance, Financial Resources, and Legal Resources Tuition Benefits: Public Loan Forgiveness Book scholarships 100% tuition coverage for up to 18 credit hours per fiscal year (other rules may apply). Retirement : Teacher Retirement System of Texas (TRS) or Optional Retirement Plan (ORP) Voluntary Tax Deferred Account/Deferred Compensation Plan Time Off : 8+ hours of vacation paid time off every month. 8 hours of sick leave time off every month. 8 hours of paid time off for Birthday leave. 12-15 paid holidays each year. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Assistant-Professor--Kinesiology---Athletic-Training_R-097210-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Kinesiology - Athletic Training",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-18\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Title Assistant Professor, Kinesiology - Athletic Training Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department COEHD-Kinesiology Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Faculty Job Description The College of Education and Human Development invites applications for a full-time , 9-month Tenure-Track position at the rank of Assistant Professor, Kinesiology - Athletic Training   to begin as early as Fall 2027 . We are seeking a dynamic faculty member to assist in the continued development of a Commission on Accreditation of Athletic Training Education (CAATE) accredited Athletic Training Program. This position will require teaching, research or creative activity, and service. This position will serve as the third faculty line for the Master of Science in Athletic Training program. The main responsibilities of this position will include, but are not limited to:  Teaching master’s level Athletic Training classes in a CAATE accredited AT Program and maintaining compliance with CAATE accreditation standards Teaching in an accredited graduate Athletic Training program and an undergraduate kinesiology program designed to help students enter allied health professions Academic advising, mentorship, and research supervision for students in a MSAT Program Supporting activities and programs arranged for the recruitment, selection, and retention of students for the MSAT Program Assisting in the completion of necessary annual and self-study accreditation reports for CAATE, SACS-COC, and the university Active engagement in scholarship & creative activity Providing service to the university, community, and profession Additional duties as assigned The successful candidate will report to the Director of the Athletic Training Program and the Chair of the Department of Kinesiology & Military Science.  Texas A&M University-Corpus Christi is a vibrant, Hispanic and Minority Serving Doctoral Research Institution that proudly provides a solid academic reputation, renowned faculty, and highly rated degree programs since 1947. The University has a heritage of teaching excellence with innovation in research and community engagement as part of the distinguished Texas A&M System. TAMU-CC is the only university in the nation located on its own island, at the heart of the Texas Gulf Coast. With palm tree-lined pathways throughout the campus, nearby natural wetlands, a scenic hike-and-bike trail, and pristine views of the beach and bays, Texas A&M University-Corpus Christi is a first-choice institution. For more information about the Department of Kinesiology & the Athletic Training Program can be found at: http://kinesiology.tamucc.edu/index.html Required Qualifications : A doctoral degree in Athletic Training, Exercise Science, Kinesiology, Curriculum and Instruction, or applicable field. Advanced ABD will be considered; degree must be conferred at the time of appointment. Proven record of dedicated line of scholarship, including recent active engagement, publications, and/or potential for on-going research and other scholarly and creative activities Proven record in or interest in teaching in athletic training or applicable field. Current Board of Certification (BOC) certification  Current membership in the National Athletic Trainers’ Association (NATA) Proof of NPI number with appropriate health care field designation Eligibility for Texas AT Licensure or Current Texas AT License Documented clinical experience in the field of Athletic Training Preferred Qualifications Documented fundable line of scholarship and/or record of receipt of grants Evidence of successful graduate and/or undergraduate teaching in a CAATE accredited Athletic Training Program (previous 3-5 years) Experience working with the Commission on Accreditation of Athletic Training Education (CAATE) Current knowledge of Athletic Training literature and CAATE requirements   Evidence of outstanding organizational skills; as well as excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills Demonstrated commitment to global competency with the skills and experience to work with individuals from various backgrounds Knowledge and experience concerning Board of Certification (BOC) Approved Provider status CPR/AED/First Aid Professional Rescuer Instructor or BLS Instructor TO APPLY:  https://www.tamucc.edu/human-resources/careers/index.php All required documentation must be submitted to be considered for the position. A completed application will include: A cover letter that addresses the qualifications listed A curriculum vitae A statement of teaching philosophy A statement of research philosophy Proof of Current BOC certification Proof of NPI number with appropriate health care field designation Proof of Current NATA Membership Copies of unofficial transcripts for graduate coursework A list of three (3) professional references, including name, address, title, telephone number, and email address Application Deadline: Monday, October 12th, 2026 Position Start Date: Fall 2027 Salary: $7,127.56 Monthly NOTE: Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. For more information, please contact the search chair, Dr. Mikaela Boham , at Mikaela.Boham@tamucc.edu . All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate---Phytoplankton-Ecology_R-097274": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate - Phytoplankton Ecology",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Title Postdoctoral Research Associate - Phytoplankton Ecology Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department Harte Research Institute Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Staff Job Description TAMU-CC is a dynamic university designated as both a Hispanic-Serving Institution (HSI) and Minority-Serving Institution (MSI) with approximately 11,000 students from 47 states and 54 foreign nations. We employ over 1,400 full-time and 2,000 part-time Islanders (including students/GAs). The University attracts highly talented faculty and staff and offers an array of undergraduate and graduate degrees, including doctoral programs.  As a member of the Texas A&M University System, TAMU-CC benefits from a range of resources, increased visibility and influence, and opportunities to collaborate in mutually beneficial ways with peers across member institutions and associated agencies.   TAMU-CC’s beautiful campus is located on a 240-acre island on Corpus Christi Bay and was ranked #1 College by the Sea by Best College Reviews. Our natural setting is enhanced by its modern, attractive, and state-of-the-art classroom buildings and support facilities. PURPOSE A postdoctoral research associate position is available in the Coastal Water Health lab at the Harte Research Institute. The postdoctoral researcher will conduct research under the auspices of a new NOAA ECOHAB grant that focuses on understanding dynamics of Aureoumbra lagunensis (“brown tide”) blooms in the Baffin Bay-Upper Laguna Madre ecosystem of Texas. The postdoctoral researcher will gain experience in project management, project development, mentoring (of undergraduate/graduate students) and stakeholder engagement. Opportunities also exist for comparative analysis of phytoplankton dynamics between estuarine systems that vary in e.g., freshwater inflow, nutrient loadings, etc. DESCRIPTION Functional Area 1: Project Coordination Percent Effort: 10% Coordinate project activities in collaboration with PI, Co-PIs and assigned staff Implement project activities   Functional Area 2: Data Acquisition Percent Effort: 40% Participate in field and laboratory-based project activities to acquire data Process and analyze samples collected Develop new techniques as appropriate for project   Functional Area 3: Research and Data Analysis Percent Effort: 40% Analyze water quality data Submit data to a data manager in a timely manner Publish results in peer reviewed journals and present at local/national/international meetings.   Functional Area 4: Research Development Percent Effort: 10% Develop new avenues of research Submit grant applications to support new avenues of research   QUALIFICATIONS Ph.D. in Marine Science or related field Experience with phytoplankton identification, preferably including marine taxa. Fundamental knowledge of phytoplankton ecology Intermediate skills in Microsoft Office Suite (Word, Excel, PowerPoint, and Outlook). Excellent verbal and written communication skills, including experience communicating scientific findings to a broad audience. Desire to work with a broad spectrum of collaborators, managers, and stakeholders. Experience with project management and ability to meet project goals in a timely manner. Ability to participate in field work under occasional harsh conditions such as heat and wind Experience with appropriate statistical techniques for analyzing time-series data and community structure data   PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience with R software packages SALARY: $4,166.67 - $4,791.67 Monthly ($50,000 - $57,500 Annually Approximately) NOTE: This a grant-funded position. Continued employment contingent upon available funding . Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. BENEFITS (rules, policies, eligibility apply) From our generous benefits package and professional development opportunities, to our retirement programs and our commitment to service excellence, the Island University is an engaging and rewarding place to work. INSURANCE: Medical $0 - $30 per month for Employee Only coverage after university contribution ($920 value). Up to 83% of premium covered by the university: Employee and Spouse Employee and Children Employee and Family coverage Dental & Vision Life Insurance, Accidental D&D, Long Term Disability, Flexible Spending Account and Day Care Spending Account DUAL CAREER PARTNER PROGRAM ON-CAMPUS WELLNESS OPPORTUNITIES Work Life Solutions: Counseling, Work Life Assistance, Financial Resources, and Legal Resources Tuition Benefits: Public Loan Forgiveness Book scholarships 100% tuition coverage for up to 18 credit hours per fiscal year (other rules may apply). Retirement : Teacher Retirement System of Texas (TRS) or Optional Retirement Plan (ORP) Voluntary Tax Deferred Account/Deferred Compensation Plan Time Off : 8+ hours of vacation paid time off every month. 8 hours of sick leave time off every month. 8 hours of paid time off for Birthday leave. 12-15 paid holidays each year. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Purchasing-Specialist_R-097234-1": {
      "title": "Purchasing Specialist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Title Purchasing Specialist Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department Procurements And Disbursements Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Staff Job Description TAMU-CC is a dynamic university designated as both a Hispanic-Serving Institution (HSI) and Minority-Serving Institution (MSI) with approximately 11,000 students from 47 states and 54 foreign nations. We employ over 1,400 full-time and 2,000 part-time Islanders (including students/GAs). The University attracts highly talented faculty and staff and offers an array of undergraduate and graduate degrees, including doctoral programs.  As a member of the Texas A&M University System, TAMU-CC benefits from a range of resources, increased visibility and influence, and opportunities to collaborate in mutually beneficial ways with peers across member institutions and associated agencies.   TAMU-CC’s beautiful campus is located on a 240-acre island on Corpus Christi Bay and was ranked #1 College by the Sea by Best College Reviews. Our natural setting is enhanced by its modern, attractive, and state-of-the-art classroom buildings and support facilities. PURPOSE The Purchasing Specialist assists in managing all aspects, to include TAMU System, State and local policies and procedures related to the Travel and Non-Travel Programs, one-card and declining balance cards, Voyager Fleet fuel and maintenance credit cards, student travel, foreign travel, and cash advance allowances. The Procurement Specialist will assist with travel and non-travel inquiries, and assist in resolving issues, conduct monthly reconciliations, prepare and disseminate assigned and/or requested reports. Review and update departmental guidebooks and web pages. Conduct training as needed.     DESCRIPTION Functional Area 1: Program Management: Review, Audit, and Approve Percent Effort: 60% Monitor and review all foreign travel requests for accuracy of required documentation, verify and attach Cultural Insurance Services International Insurance (CISI), and process visual compliance. Ensure required TrainTraq courses are complete. Monitor and review all student travel packets for accuracy and required documentation related to the student travel packet. Update Laserfiche, Excel spreadsheet, and student travel log. Coordinate with department and UPD to obtain driving clearance, when necessary. Communicate any findings with the department. Review One-Card and Declining Balance (DBAL) applications for accuracy and required information.  Order new cards through the credit card bank software, prepare One-Card and DBal Receipt and Cardholder Agreement forms for distribution to cardholders. Update required spreadsheets in Laserfiche. Assist cardholders with declines or credit card issues. Responsible for updating credit limit (increase/decrease) as requested by department. Cancel DBal credit cards upon return of trip. Review and monitor Amazon Business account applications, maintain and update user and group accounts. Functional Area 2: Administrative: Reconciliation Percent Effort: 20% Perform monthly reconciliation of Emburse unallowed charges for Travel and non-travel, and cash advance allowances.   Review and disseminate past due transactions to department on a weekly basis to reduce the number of outstanding items. Assist departments with inquiries related to outstanding transactions. Review backup and prepare payment request for Voyager/US Bank Fleet Management System. Responsible for maintaining inventory data updated with new and deleted vehicles in the Voyager Fleet Management System. Coordinate with departments, audit and research declined issues, revise credit limits as requested by department(s). Review and verify travel and non-travel reimbursement requests.   Functional Area 3: Support: Reporting and other duties Percent Effort: 20% Disseminate monthly outstanding reconciliation reports to departments. Assist departments with inquiries related to past due transactions. Maintain Voyager Fleet Management data and submit quarterly inventory reports to the State. Complete, provide, and disseminate monthly reports to Administration. Act as a backup for Purchasing Department front office; answer phones, greet visitors, and forward email inquiries to appropriate individuals. Monitor Travel and Non-Travel and other assigned email inbox.  Conduct training as needed; one-on-one or scheduled quarterly training. Perform other duties as necessary and/or assigned. QUALIFICATIONS High School Diploma / GED Two (2) years of clerical, accounts payable, or related professional experience. Additional education may be considered as a substitute for the minimum required experience: Associate's degree. Intermediate skills in Microsoft Office (Excel, Word, PowerPoint, and Outlook) Excellent written and verbal communications and customer service skills. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Associates degree in Business, Accounting, or closely related field, or specialized/technical training with some college courses Experience in clerical work in a University or State Purchasing department. Experience with e-Travel/Emburse, Manner, Cascade, Visual Compliance, Outlook email system, FAMIS, or similar automated system. SALARY: Up to $17.27/hourly Up to $35,921.60/annually, approximately NOTE: Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. BENEFITS (rules, policies, eligibility apply) From our generous benefits package and professional development opportunities, to our retirement programs and our commitment to service excellence, the Island University is an engaging and rewarding place to work. Insurance Medical $0 - $30 per month for Employee Only coverage after university contribution ($920 value). Up to 83% of premium covered by the university: Employee and Spouse Employee and Children Employee and Family coverage Dental & Vision Life Insurance, Accidental D&D, Long Term Disability, Flexible Spending Account and Day Care Spending Account On-campus wellness opportunities Work Life Solutions: Counseling, Work Life Assistance, Financial Resources, and Legal Resources Tuition Benefits: Public Loan Forgiveness Book scholarships 100% tuition coverage for up to 18 credit hours per fiscal year (other rules may apply). Retirement Teacher Retirement System of Texas (TRS) or Optional Retirement Plan (ORP) Voluntary Tax Deferred Account/Deferred Compensation Plan Time Off: 8+ hours of vacation paid time off every month. 8 hours of sick leave time off every month. 8 hours of paid time off for Birthday leave. 12-15 paid holidays each year. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    },
    "https://tamus.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/TAMUCC_External/job/Corpus-Christi-TX/Records-and-Digital-Engagement-Specialist_R-097486-1": {
      "title": "Records and Digital Engagement Specialist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-25\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nJob Title Records and Digital Engagement Specialist Agency Texas A&M University - Corpus Christi Department Office Of The Registrar Proposed Minimum Salary Commensurate Job Location Corpus Christi, Texas Job Type Staff Job Description TAMU-CC is a dynamic university designated as both a Hispanic-Serving Institution (HSI) and Minority-Serving Institution (MSI) with approximately 11,000 students from 47 states and 54 foreign nations. We employ over 1,400 full-time and 2,000 part-time Islanders (including students/GAs). The University attracts highly talented faculty and staff and offers an array of undergraduate and graduate degrees, including doctoral programs.  As a member of the Texas A&M University System, TAMU-CC benefits from a range of resources, increased visibility and influence, and opportunities to collaborate in mutually beneficial ways with peers across member institutions and associated agencies.   TAMU-CC’s beautiful campus is located on a 240-acre island on Corpus Christi Bay and was ranked #1 College by the Sea by Best College Reviews. Our natural setting is enhanced by its modern, attractive, and state-of-the-art classroom buildings and support facilities. PURPOSE The Records and Digital Engagement Specialist, under general supervision, primary purpose is to set up, control, and maintain University files. This position assists in developing and updating records, as well as departmental and university policies and procedures.  This position is also responsible for the graphic design of various marketing electronic and physical deliverables to be used for various campaigns along with the management of departmental webpages and social media accounts.   DESCRIPTION Functional Area 1: Operations Percent Effort: 45% Create an effective and efficient student-centered, service-oriented atmosphere and provide excellent customer service. This includes, but not limited to, answering the telephone promptly; processing students’ changes to the student records accurately and timely. Answer multi-line telephones and troubleshoot any issues presented by callers. Ensures compliance with academic, regulatory requirements, including adherence to FERPA guidelines, accuracy, timeliness, and student-centered professionalism. Provide assistance with registration, transcripts, enrollment certifications and verifications, and end of term processes.     Functional Area 2: Marketing, Communications & Digital Engagement Percent Effort: 35%   Serve as the liaison for Marketing and Communications on behalf of the Registrar’s Office. Assist in promotion and placement of stories with appropriate electronic media, including approved social media ventures. Assist in the development, design and updates of the Registrar web pages. Create, implement, research, and maintain e-marketing initiatives to better communicate with customer bases, via email marketing, social media, virtual environments, etc. Deploy and maintain ongoing efforts for optimized, strategic, branded presences in select social media. Monitor brand mention with a focus on developing, connecting, and engaging with strong communities with a dedicated and responsive outreach. Create/design graphics for use in various communication populations for use in marketing campaigns. Regularly update department webpages with up-to-date information and documents. Create email/text messages templates for the communication of important information and deadlines. Collaborate with partners across campus to successfully execute campaigns.     Functional Area 3:  Administrative Support Percent Effort: 20%   Assist with commencement, and Registrar related events throughout the year. Assist Associate Registrar with special projects and perform other duties as assigned.   QUALIFICATIONS Associate’s degree in applicable field. Three (3) years of related experience. Additional experience may be used as a substitute for the minimum education requirement: High School Diploma or GED and five (5) years of related experience Excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills to be able to communicate effectively in a positive, courteous, professional manner with varying population of customers in person, telephone, or e-mail. Self-motivated, self-directed, and has excellent attention to detail. Ability to work outside regular business hours and weekends as needed for commencement and University programs. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor's degree in Communication or related field. Experience in admissions and records in higher education. Working knowledge of Banner Student Information System. Experience with Adobe Suite or Canva Knowledge of Cascade Experience with Mongoose Cadence engagement platform Up to $17.93 Hourly (Up to $37,437.84 Annually, Approximately) NOTE:   Employment may be impacted by the Presidential proclamation issued on September 19, 2025 and the moratorium issued by Texas Governor Abbott on January 27, 2026. BENEFITS (rules, policies, eligibility apply) From our generous benefits package and professional development opportunities, to our retirement programs and our commitment to service excellence, the Island University is an engaging and rewarding place to work. INSURANCE: Medical$0 - $30 per month for Employee Only coverage after university contribution ($920 value).Up to 83% of premium covered by the university:Employee and SpouseEmployee and ChildrenEmployee and Family coverage Dental & Vision Life Insurance, Accidental D&D, Long Term Disability, Flexible Spending Account and Day Care Spending Account DUAL CAREER PARTNER PROGRAM ON-CAMPUS WELLNESS OPPORTUNITIES Work Life Solutions: Counseling, Work Life Assistance, Financial Resources, and Legal Resources Tuition Benefits: Public Loan Forgiveness Book scholarships 100% tuition coverage for up to 18 credit hours per fiscal year (other rules may apply). Retirement : Teacher Retirement System of Texas (TRS) or Optional Retirement Plan (ORP) Voluntary Tax Deferred Account/Deferred Compensation Plan Time Off : 8+ hours of vacation paid time off every month. 8 hours of sick leave time off every month. 8 hours of paid time off for Birthday leave. 12-15 paid holidays each year. All positions are security-sensitive. Applicants are subject to a criminal history investigation, and employment is contingent upon the institution’s verification of credentials and/or other information required by the institution’s procedures, including the completion of the criminal history check. Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer."
    }
  },
